how do you ensure owner occupancy? Because I think one of the

01:58:01.697 --> 01:58:05.385
questions that we have is, how does this not get bought up by

01:58:05.705 --> 01:58:09.556
investors that then, you know, resell it or rent it

01:58:09.764 --> 01:58:12.347
at a higher value? How do you ensure that the people that are going to

01:58:12.847 --> 01:58:16.667
be living there are the employees in

01:58:16.684 --> 01:58:19.539
Guelph that are meant to be having this housing?

01:58:20.346 --> 01:58:22.750
Through Your Worship to the Councillor,

01:58:23.476 --> 01:58:26.876
by agreement, and I think Michael Bay will talk

01:58:27.376 --> 01:58:30.236
to that in presentation. Okay,

01:58:30.252 --> 01:58:33.732
okay, I appreciate that. So I will follow up

01:58:34.232 --> 01:58:37.148
with Mr. Bay on that. On July 14th, when there was a presentation as part

01:58:37.648 --> 01:58:40.850
of your submission, it talked about 470 affordable units,

01:58:41.414 --> 01:58:44.425
uh, 50% of the 940 total proposed units.

01:58:44.956 --> 01:58:48.708
Can you help me understand what— how you define the affordable units

01:58:48.837 --> 01:58:51.722
when you talk about the half of these units are going to be affordable?

01:58:53.312 --> 01:58:57.057
I will transfer that to Mike to give the further breakdown of that.

01:58:58.191 --> 01:59:01.336
Okay, I'll hold my questions for Mike. Thank you, Mr. Hapka.

01:59:01.433 --> 01:59:04.473
At least they know what's coming. Councillor Downar.

01:59:05.112 --> 01:59:08.459
Thank you. Through you, Mr. Mayor, Mr. Handy, so I—

01:59:08.748 --> 01:59:11.975
so you said you, you thought that you could get

01:59:12.475 --> 01:59:16.599
there through the alternative. What is that? So a concern

01:59:16.632 --> 01:59:20.838
I have is that if we— so

01:59:20.887 --> 01:59:24.339
staff are recommending refusal, right? And correct. And then,

01:59:24.355 --> 01:59:27.662
but, and then they have an alternative motion. If we

01:59:28.162 --> 01:59:32.496
approve the alternative motion How

01:59:32.641 --> 01:59:35.619
will you— you don't— how would you get there through the alternative?

01:59:37.739 --> 01:59:40.177
Through— through to the councillor,

01:59:41.662 --> 01:59:45.602
the things that I talked about this evening are things that probably

01:59:45.634 --> 01:59:49.676
should be massaged further, either through council's

01:59:50.176 --> 01:59:53.573
debate and direction to staff or through

01:59:54.042 --> 01:59:57.958
working with staff further, and they've been quite good to entertain meetings

01:59:58.050 --> 02:00:01.273
and things to even get to this point. So we don't agree

02:00:01.353 --> 02:00:05.186
on everything, but I'll have to say that the alternative was

02:00:05.234 --> 02:00:08.650
a good step forward. Even the mapping change, we thought, okay,

02:00:08.730 --> 02:00:12.162
we had talked about that earlier, but that's a good thing. And there's some other

02:00:12.194 --> 02:00:15.738
schedules that are just more implementation to

02:00:15.882 --> 02:00:19.795
natural heritage. So there are things that— so I'm not sure what

02:00:20.295 --> 02:00:22.698
this looks like at the moment, to be honest. And I've been involved in a

02:00:23.198 --> 02:00:26.694
lot of applications in the city. To know whether we can live

02:00:27.194 --> 02:00:30.724
edit this, whether it comes back, whether, you know, we could do

02:00:31.224 --> 02:00:34.706
that. So maybe that's a long answer, but— So that's kind of my concern,

02:00:34.819 --> 02:00:38.207
right? Because I think a lot of us here would like to see

02:00:38.707 --> 02:00:42.028
affordable housing, and a lot of us might support

02:00:42.528 --> 02:00:44.710
the alternative that is there.

02:00:45.480 --> 02:00:48.884
But if we come down either on a hard no, or if some

02:00:49.384 --> 02:00:52.685
of us can't, accept some of those changes in a decision-making

02:00:52.877 --> 02:00:56.227
room. So I guess my background as a mediator is coming out.

02:00:56.484 --> 02:00:59.946
Like, is this not best referred back to—

02:01:00.956 --> 02:01:05.364
if, if we can't come to an— that kind of consensus

02:01:05.864 --> 02:01:09.115
here, is it better for you to take more time working with staff on this

02:01:09.853 --> 02:01:13.539
rather than have coming down on a hard decision tonight?

02:01:13.619 --> 02:01:17.022
If you're saying we think we can get there, we've been working through with

02:01:17.522 --> 02:01:20.781
staff, which is great. I'm just asking because,

02:01:20.878 --> 02:01:24.256
like, I'm concerned about, you know, not being able to—

02:01:24.272 --> 02:01:28.152
like, depending on what staff says about some of these things that you don't agree

02:01:28.652 --> 02:01:32.051
with right now, that it might not be palatable for enough councillors

02:01:32.166 --> 02:01:33.052
to support.

02:01:35.982 --> 02:01:39.836
So I'm just sort of asking you, which is a better scenario

02:01:40.336 --> 02:01:44.000
for you? Well, ideally, we'd like a decision made

02:01:44.433 --> 02:01:47.878
this evening by this council, uh, to, to be, you know,

02:01:47.894 --> 02:01:50.827
straightforward. But I also don't want to, uh,

02:01:50.843 --> 02:01:54.368
make a decision that we're all not comfortable with. Whether there's

02:01:54.868 --> 02:01:58.102
any timing between this council meeting— and I know your last council meeting,

02:01:58.198 --> 02:02:01.596
which makes this a difficult year, is next week, and I know that

02:02:02.096 --> 02:02:05.746
agenda is pretty full as, as well— so whether there

02:02:06.246 --> 02:02:09.593
could be anything or direction out of this, uh, to come

02:02:10.093 --> 02:02:13.819
to a solution in this term of council.

02:02:15.137 --> 02:02:18.544
And of course, I'm not looking what my client is either nodding or

02:02:19.548 --> 02:02:23.449
behind me. I'm answering this as best I can at the moment.

02:02:23.595 --> 02:02:26.735
Yeah, they left the room. Yeah, sorry, I'm all on my own.

02:02:27.850 --> 02:02:32.156
Okay, thank you. All right, so Hugh,

02:02:32.172 --> 02:02:35.706
thank you very much. I think it would be good to hear as the next

02:02:36.206 --> 02:02:39.894
delegations come up and then If anyone's questions align with that delegation, we can go

02:02:40.394 --> 02:02:44.923
from there. So Mike LeBay from Home Opportunities Nonprofit Corporation is

02:02:45.261 --> 02:02:49.278
here, and then I really hope I'm saying your name right. Is it, is it

02:02:49.778 --> 02:02:53.520
Tywin? Am I saying that right? Did I? Okay, uh, Tywin Thomas,

02:02:54.018 --> 02:02:56.975
uh, you're in the wings. Okay, Mike, welcome.

02:02:57.087 --> 02:03:00.124
Thank you for coming, and whenever you start, you have your 5 minutes. Go right

02:03:00.624 --> 02:03:04.541
ahead. Yes, thank you, Mr. Mayor, and fellow other and

02:03:04.621 --> 02:03:07.783
council. Um, what I want to

02:03:08.283 --> 02:03:12.051
start by saying is, uh, I've been doing these things for a long time,

02:03:12.100 --> 02:03:16.096
and I've never seen the kind of innovation and flexibility that comes with an alternative

02:03:16.596 --> 02:03:20.188
report along with a, a report. So I was very pleased to

02:03:20.688 --> 02:03:24.457
see that, and I think, uh, staff should be commended for having put that kind

02:03:24.473 --> 02:03:26.800
of thinking together for this particular report.

02:03:27.891 --> 02:03:30.594
The other thing I want to say is that for 30 years now,

02:03:32.340 --> 02:03:36.387
through Options for Homes, then Home Opportunities, my career has

02:03:36.501 --> 02:03:39.869
been totally focused on keeping investors out of our projects.

02:03:41.100 --> 02:03:44.562
We have a number of techniques, including requiring the property

02:03:45.062 --> 02:03:48.466
manager to tell us if there's a renter in the building. That's in

02:03:48.579 --> 02:03:52.835
their contract, it's required in our agreement with the condominium,

02:03:53.030 --> 02:03:56.105
and so we have that mechanism to identify where the

02:03:56.605 --> 02:03:59.946
renters are and if they're renting, they're in

02:03:59.978 --> 02:04:02.721
default under the mortgage that we have on their property.

02:04:03.250 --> 02:04:06.922
So we have a right to sell their property from under them if they're renting

02:04:07.098 --> 02:04:10.482
inappropriately. Now, I should say we're not preventing people

02:04:10.982 --> 02:04:14.458
from renting for 3 months because they're going for a long trip. It's about somebody

02:04:14.958 --> 02:04:18.163
who's actually turning it into renting. So we have that mechanism built

02:04:18.243 --> 02:04:21.787
into what we're doing, and that agreement to do

02:04:22.287 --> 02:04:26.007
that can be put into an agreement with the city, so the city can

02:04:26.103 --> 02:04:30.438
backstop us. What's being recommended, and the reason we've suggested

02:04:30.938 --> 02:04:34.757
a change from that, is otherwise you're going to have 400 units where

02:04:34.854 --> 02:04:38.129
the city has a direct relationship with them, where they have

02:04:38.161 --> 02:04:41.373
to check constantly on what they're doing, and if the

02:04:41.614 --> 02:04:44.729
people are uncomfortable, they're going to go to their councillor and go to the mayor

02:04:44.745 --> 02:04:48.807
and complain, and you guys don't want to be involved. Sign an obligation

02:04:48.839 --> 02:04:52.336
to us there'll be consequences if we don't maintain

02:04:52.368 --> 02:04:55.172
the obligation, and we'll do the administration on your behalf.

02:04:55.832 --> 02:04:59.474
So, that may help the question. I'm happy to take more if

02:04:59.974 --> 02:05:03.293
necessary. Now, which button am I pushing?

02:05:08.056 --> 02:05:09.801
Ah, the only one I didn't push.

02:05:11.670 --> 02:05:15.837
Yeah, good. So, I've got brief number

02:05:16.337 --> 02:05:19.758
of slides, and of course the beginning was

02:05:19.855 --> 02:05:22.049
answering a question, but anyways, never mind, I won't ask one more time.

02:05:23.213 --> 02:05:26.186
I just wanted to show you the types of people that we're trying to reach,

02:05:26.973 --> 02:05:31.377
and it's the CMHC definition that allows us to reach those people. The unfortunate

02:05:31.409 --> 02:05:34.970
thing about the provincial definition is that it's just a

02:05:35.470 --> 02:05:38.697
number. It's a number that works for bachelor and

02:05:39.197 --> 02:05:42.917
studio units. I don't want to build just bachelor and studio units.

02:05:43.356 --> 02:05:46.421
I want to get a family with $85,000 into

02:05:46.921 --> 02:05:51.139
a 3-bedroom apartment or 4-bedroom townhouse. They don't qualify

02:05:51.155 --> 02:05:54.477
under the definition because the definition isn't appropriate to all

02:05:54.605 --> 02:05:58.104
types of housing. So, so it's important for us to

02:05:58.136 --> 02:06:01.506
serve the people I want to serve, which involves children. And so

02:06:02.006 --> 02:06:05.662
that's why we've gone to 10% provincial, 23% CMHC.

02:06:06.063 --> 02:06:09.449
The provincial will actually also fall under CMHC because it's more rigorous and

02:06:09.949 --> 02:06:13.646
more affordable than the provincial definition. And we've talked

02:06:14.146 --> 02:06:18.788
about the issue of

02:06:19.047 --> 02:06:22.284
providing the additional 17%. I think maybe

02:06:22.316 --> 02:06:25.881
it comes on the next slide. This I've just focused on.

02:06:26.872 --> 02:06:30.055
One of the best things that can be done in Guelph

02:06:30.555 --> 02:06:33.277
at this point in time is to build more housing. Housing produces jobs.

02:06:34.106 --> 02:06:37.247
Build more housing through Kiwi Newton, who happens to have a relationship with

02:06:37.747 --> 02:06:41.703
Linamar, if Linamar has people that are losing jobs, if we get

02:06:42.203 --> 02:06:46.191
enough housing going, Kiwi Inuit can hire them. So it becomes a very defensive position

02:06:46.369 --> 02:06:48.324
for the city to take.

02:06:51.640 --> 02:06:54.290
So yes, so the 33% includes the 10%.

02:06:55.544 --> 02:06:59.303
It is more affordable than you'd get if you had 33% under the provincial definition,

02:06:59.367 --> 02:07:02.876
which is why we're asking you to recognize it today. 'cause staff is tied up

02:07:03.376 --> 02:07:06.858
without you doing that. If you don't do it, they can't work with us on

02:07:06.890 --> 02:07:10.102
that. 17% will work with staff. We've talked

02:07:10.602 --> 02:07:13.763
about the job growth. Contributes to your housing targets,

02:07:14.678 --> 02:07:17.777
supports local employers and attracts new ones,

02:07:18.371 --> 02:07:21.759
mitigates the impacts of tariffs. Very quickly, I really get

02:07:22.259 --> 02:07:25.982
excited about this. We're supposed to be able— we're at risk of losing 100,000

02:07:26.482 --> 02:07:28.836
jobs. Due to the tariff situation.

02:07:30.769 --> 02:07:34.552
The 200,000 units of affordable housing that this country is supposed to build produces

02:07:35.052 --> 02:07:39.031
300,000 jobs. So it's a mechanism to

02:07:39.161 --> 02:07:41.804
thumb our nose at the trumped-up stuff down south.

02:07:43.584 --> 02:07:45.774
I can ask— answer more questions on affordability.

02:07:47.790 --> 02:07:50.514
Okay, thank you very much for your presentation. We appreciate it.

02:07:51.433 --> 02:07:54.706
And questions? I'll start with, uh, see, uh, Councillor Caron,

02:07:54.834 --> 02:07:57.969
and then I'll go, uh, Goller, and then I'll go to the screen

02:07:58.162 --> 02:08:01.475
to Kayton after that. Okay, thanks. And thanks for describing the

02:08:01.975 --> 02:08:05.770
model because it's very much a part of the decision-making in terms of assuring

02:08:05.802 --> 02:08:09.485
that we're, we're getting that affordability that we're so desperate

02:08:09.985 --> 02:08:15.401
for in this community. I hear a lot from condominium

02:08:15.450 --> 02:08:19.594
corporations that it's the maintenance fees or the condo fees

02:08:20.205 --> 02:08:24.104
or the utilities that are actually impacting affordability. They can buy into

02:08:24.604 --> 02:08:27.369
the market but they can't afford to maintain and operate and pay the utilities.

02:08:27.867 --> 02:08:31.656
Can you speak to affordability when those costs are added and

02:08:32.156 --> 02:08:36.312
whether the formula is still working in, in your calculations?

02:08:37.019 --> 02:08:40.214
Yes, an extremely good question. One,

02:08:40.712 --> 02:08:44.325
using Kiwi Newton, they build nearly to Passive House

02:08:44.825 --> 02:08:48.339
standards, so the energy use in the

02:08:48.355 --> 02:08:51.549
buildings will be reduced. Which allows for more money to

02:08:52.049 --> 02:08:55.943
carry mortgage money, because we're hired by the future owners of a condominium

02:08:56.039 --> 02:08:59.663
to build an affordable unit, and affordable is their entire carrying costs.

02:09:00.369 --> 02:09:03.689
So if we reduce the utilities, they actually have more money to spend on a

02:09:04.189 --> 02:09:06.976
mortgage. The more they— the larger the mortgage they have, the more money they make

02:09:07.024 --> 02:09:10.953
themselves. So we're actually helping them out because they only make their profit

02:09:11.034 --> 02:09:14.642
on the portion they carry. On the maintenance

02:09:15.142 --> 02:09:18.574
side, the condo fees That's a good thing, by the way, because the

02:09:19.074 --> 02:09:22.830
condo legislation is quite serious. You have to have a good replacement

02:09:22.846 --> 02:09:25.978
reserve, so if something goes wrong, you can fix it. It doesn't become a crisis

02:09:26.478 --> 02:09:30.121
in the condominium. But also, we don't put in pools, we don't put in saunas,

02:09:30.218 --> 02:09:33.703
we don't put expensive amenities that will add to that cost.

02:09:34.072 --> 02:09:38.199
So we're usually on the maintenance side about

02:09:38.264 --> 02:09:41.411
20% lower than a regular condo. So we,

02:09:41.476 --> 02:09:44.769
we pay attention to all of those expenses expenses. And we will

02:09:44.914 --> 02:09:49.198
be— actually, we put out newsletters every 6 months after

02:09:49.698 --> 02:09:52.092
people have moved in, and we'll be giving them tips on how to keep costs

02:09:52.592 --> 02:09:55.899
down. Thank you.

02:09:56.028 --> 02:09:59.231
Uh, Goler, then Keaton, please. Uh, thank you,

02:09:59.279 --> 02:10:02.158
Your Worship. Uh, thank you so much, Mr. LeBay. Um,

02:10:02.692 --> 02:10:06.309
I'll go back to the question that I had about, uh, the presentation in July.

02:10:06.799 --> 02:10:10.155
You said that of the 940 proposed units $470 would

02:10:10.203 --> 02:10:13.656
be affordable. Can you help me understand, because now the presentation says

02:10:13.752 --> 02:10:17.205
that, uh, 10% would meet the provincial definition,

02:10:17.479 --> 02:10:20.849
another 23% would be the CMHC definition.

02:10:21.369 --> 02:10:24.668
What about that remaining 17%? How would those be defined?

02:10:25.212 --> 02:10:28.995
We intend the other 17%, after discussion with staff,

02:10:29.011 --> 02:10:30.627
to also meet the CMHC definition.

02:10:32.405 --> 02:10:36.268
Okay, okay. So, so in effect, it would be 40% meet the CMHC

02:10:36.768 --> 02:10:39.765
definition and 10% meet the provincial definition. That's where we want to get to,

02:10:40.840 --> 02:10:44.625
and whatever we agree with your staff on is our minimum

02:10:45.125 --> 02:10:48.475
commitment. Okay. In other locations, we've been able to do— get

02:10:48.975 --> 02:10:52.181
it up to 60 or 70, but I'm— don't, don't say I've

02:10:52.681 --> 02:10:56.159
said 60 or 70, right? I've said 50 in working out with staff,

02:10:56.641 --> 02:11:00.266
but my goal as a corporation is to produce

02:11:00.330 --> 02:11:03.410
as much affordable housing as possible and to have

02:11:03.910 --> 02:11:07.951
it last in perpetuity. Now, I want to mention that just clearly so

02:11:07.967 --> 02:11:12.106
it's stated. In perpetuity to us is if we put 470

02:11:12.138 --> 02:11:14.994
units of affordability under the CMHC definition,

02:11:15.973 --> 02:11:19.310
in perpetuity there will be 470 units in Guelph

02:11:19.823 --> 02:11:23.192
for that income group. It won't necessarily be

02:11:23.692 --> 02:11:27.059
on this site because when someone sells, we take the money and put another unit

02:11:27.559 --> 02:11:31.361
for the same income somewhere else. But we've committed to maintain

02:11:31.458 --> 02:11:34.845
470, and then the next project we add to that. So the

02:11:35.345 --> 02:11:38.217
portfolio protects the affordability, not the individual units,

02:11:38.522 --> 02:11:42.215
which is also why we tend not to put controls on title

02:11:42.552 --> 02:11:45.859
but controls through mortgages. Okay, okay,

02:11:46.148 --> 02:11:50.371
that is helpful. Then help me understand the, the staff alternate

02:11:50.419 --> 02:11:54.000
motions recommend a 49-year holding period

02:11:54.177 --> 02:11:57.869
for that affordability, what does that do to your ability

02:11:58.949 --> 02:12:02.077
to move forward the project? It makes it

02:12:02.142 --> 02:12:05.247
very difficult because what you're

02:12:05.296 --> 02:12:11.478
in essence doing is putting a control on resale that undermines—

02:12:12.073 --> 02:12:15.213
today it wouldn't matter. There's no investors out there.

02:12:15.519 --> 02:12:19.220
They don't see enough revenue to bother. But when investors are in

02:12:19.237 --> 02:12:22.207
the market and looking for something, if that restriction is there,

02:12:22.522 --> 02:12:26.584
they won't be interested. We control the affordability by having a mortgage

02:12:26.776 --> 02:12:29.313
that when people sell, they give us back the mortgage and we give it to

02:12:29.329 --> 02:12:32.652
the next person. So we use a pay-it-forward scheme

02:12:32.717 --> 02:12:36.730
rather than a restrictive scheme. Pay-it-forward schemes are simpler

02:12:37.230 --> 02:12:40.647
to administer, less expensive to people during the administration, and they

02:12:41.147 --> 02:12:45.191
don't encumber somebody's ownership to any great degree

02:12:45.624 --> 02:12:48.867
after they've given us their money back. See, once they've given us their money back,

02:12:49.156 --> 02:12:52.833
What are we doing restricting their title? And so we use that money

02:12:52.865 --> 02:12:56.879
to effectively produce affordability elsewhere and control it once again

02:12:57.379 --> 02:13:00.941
through the mortgage. So it's understanding

02:13:00.973 --> 02:13:04.682
that the money keeps providing the affordability, that restricting title

02:13:04.827 --> 02:13:08.487
interferes with the proper work of the marketplace. I'm making

02:13:08.503 --> 02:13:12.164
all this work within the marketplace because we can't change that. Okay,

02:13:12.453 --> 02:13:16.079
okay, I appreciate that. And through Your Worship, Could you

02:13:16.304 --> 02:13:19.797
please help me understand, Ms. LeBay, if we were to attach

02:13:20.054 --> 02:13:23.162
a time frame, say, you know, like 25 years, we say these

02:13:23.275 --> 02:13:27.008
33% of units, we want to make sure they're affordable for 35

02:13:27.040 --> 02:13:30.630
years or 25 years. How would that even work?

02:13:30.790 --> 02:13:34.172
Because people are— these are not affordable rental units that we're

02:13:34.672 --> 02:13:38.563
capping the rent price. So how do you maintain affordability beyond

02:13:39.063 --> 02:13:41.768
the first year when someone has bought it? So what could that look like?

02:13:42.505 --> 02:13:46.048
Yeah, let me be clear that this is getting into detail

02:13:46.112 --> 02:13:49.014
that I'm happy to discuss with anybody outside of this meeting,

02:13:49.078 --> 02:13:52.412
but let me give you a brief explanation. One,

02:13:53.246 --> 02:13:56.292
we want people to do better. That's the starting point.

02:13:56.853 --> 02:14:00.988
People are qualified as affordable at the point in which they purchase

02:14:01.165 --> 02:14:05.092
the unit, so that's their income. They have— let's

02:14:05.592 --> 02:14:08.763
say that they have an $800 $400,000 4-bedroom,

02:14:09.355 --> 02:14:11.919
they can only afford to carry $400,000.

02:14:12.560 --> 02:14:15.653
Okay, that's affordable. Let's say— and

02:14:15.990 --> 02:14:19.772
it's happened to us— where there's a single mom with 3 kids occupying that

02:14:19.836 --> 02:14:21.903
unit. Well, they get married.

02:14:23.361 --> 02:14:25.589
What are you going to do, say, "Sorry, you got married, you got to leave,

02:14:25.765 --> 02:14:28.265
you're no longer an affordable qualifier"?

02:14:29.211 --> 02:14:33.057
What happens is they want to— now they've got more income— pay back

02:14:33.557 --> 02:14:37.451
the $400,000 that we lent to them, and we take that $400,000,

02:14:37.467 --> 02:14:40.226
go find another single mom with 3 kids and help her out.

02:14:41.476 --> 02:14:44.989
So, the affordability travels forward as

02:14:45.181 --> 02:14:48.404
individuals become— move into a different income

02:14:48.469 --> 02:14:51.756
category and then would— whereas in a rental unit,

02:14:51.820 --> 02:14:55.814
what happens is the rents go up and it ceases to be affordable because

02:14:55.846 --> 02:15:00.143
you're increasing the rents, but you don't help anyone else. That unit gets— disappears,

02:15:00.576 --> 02:15:04.616
right? So it's just the beauty of this model is that people

02:15:04.824 --> 02:15:06.668
give us the money back when they don't need it so we can give it

02:15:07.168 --> 02:15:10.499
to some others. Right. And help me understand, how does that work within the

02:15:10.999 --> 02:15:14.315
context of this site? Because we have 940 units.

02:15:15.084 --> 02:15:18.948
Hopefully they'll all be sold in the first year or two. So then when someone

02:15:19.733 --> 02:15:23.629
no longer qualifies for that affordability definition

02:15:23.821 --> 02:15:27.109
and they they give you the money back so that you

02:15:27.609 --> 02:15:31.214
can invest it somewhere else, there's no more units available. So does that

02:15:31.423 --> 02:15:35.175
require that then you start another project in Guelph for us to realize

02:15:35.913 --> 02:15:39.345
that affordability? That is our commitment. If within

02:15:39.521 --> 02:15:43.033
2 years of that unit selling we don't have another unit

02:15:43.130 --> 02:15:44.958
to replace it, we have to give you back the money.

02:15:46.369 --> 02:15:49.095
Okay. And then you can decide what to do with it. Trust me, you're never

02:15:49.595 --> 02:15:52.736
getting the money back. But our obligation is to give it

02:15:52.769 --> 02:15:56.410
back to you. And so, yeah, we've worked

02:15:56.442 --> 02:15:59.409
at this a long time to get it, because our goals are the same as

02:15:59.909 --> 02:16:03.388
your goals. Our intent is the same as your intent. We've just found a more

02:16:03.660 --> 02:16:06.997
refined way to make it work for

02:16:07.029 --> 02:16:10.478
everybody, because we don't want to restrict people's ability to improve their

02:16:10.978 --> 02:16:14.568
lives. We want them to improve their lives and then help someone else improve their

02:16:15.068 --> 02:16:18.417
life. Okay, okay. And just so that I'm clear, this, this model

02:16:18.917 --> 02:16:23.323
that you're referring to, where else, in which other municipalities can

02:16:23.823 --> 02:16:27.699
we see that you've done this? Well, we are currently working

02:16:27.715 --> 02:16:31.322
in about 8 municipalities. Our first

02:16:31.822 --> 02:16:35.314
project— well, first I should mention that Options for Homes has

02:16:35.346 --> 02:16:38.809
been doing this since 2000, but this is a more,

02:16:39.258 --> 02:16:42.368
more intense model that reaches lower incomes,

02:16:42.624 --> 02:16:45.814
but it uses the approach. So we've been doing that in Toronto, we did

02:16:45.862 --> 02:16:49.435
two in Guelph, we did some in Kitchener. Right now we're

02:16:49.935 --> 02:16:53.377
looking in places like Kitchener, Blue Mountains, Owen Sound,

02:16:53.521 --> 02:16:57.207
Toronto to implement this model. The first construction will happen

02:16:57.707 --> 02:17:00.556
in Brampton, and in fact the Brampton experience is what we're going to be working

02:17:00.572 --> 02:17:04.033
with staff on to use what we've already learned from there to apply

02:17:04.533 --> 02:17:07.462
here. Okay, okay, thank you very much. Thank you, Your Worship.

02:17:08.360 --> 02:17:11.272
Thank you. And I have Keaton and then, uh,

02:17:11.581 --> 02:17:12.786
Alt and then Gibson.

02:17:15.188 --> 02:17:18.664
Thank you, through you, Mr. Mayor. Um, so I've heard not in

02:17:18.697 --> 02:17:21.895
this meeting, but in the past, um, that,

02:17:24.018 --> 02:17:27.830
uh, although as a nonprofit that you qualify, um,

02:17:27.959 --> 02:17:30.613
for WAVE development fees, that,

02:17:31.279 --> 02:17:33.539
uh, you were going to voluntarily,

02:17:34.693 --> 02:17:38.639
um, pay them. So could you explain that and give any

02:17:38.671 --> 02:17:41.479
details? Yes, um,

02:17:42.041 --> 02:17:45.604
voluntarily pay them after the first ownership.

02:17:46.631 --> 02:17:50.162
So what happens is the money is used to produce affordability in the

02:17:50.662 --> 02:17:54.928
first ownership. At the end of that ownership, when the unit sells,

02:17:55.362 --> 02:17:59.165
the funding is available to pay back voluntarily to the city. We can't

02:17:59.665 --> 02:18:03.288
sign a contract to that effect because we'd be circumventing the legislation. And of course,

02:18:03.304 --> 02:18:06.735
on the next project we're working on, it will be eligible for the development

02:18:07.235 --> 02:18:11.160
charges. Now, the reason that we're doing this, to be

02:18:11.660 --> 02:18:15.201
discussed with your finance— financing department, if we promise

02:18:15.249 --> 02:18:18.904
that we're going to repay the development charges,

02:18:19.561 --> 02:18:22.608
you can take the cash flow out of your reserve account, which means you don't

02:18:23.108 --> 02:18:25.798
have to increase taxes. We don't want to be known as people increase taxes.

02:18:26.568 --> 02:18:30.560
So you can— this— take another reserve account, we'll replenish the reserve account

02:18:31.060 --> 02:18:33.841
with donations And so you don't have to increase taxes because you're going to be

02:18:34.341 --> 02:18:37.529
replenished constantly as we go forward. So it's because

02:18:37.561 --> 02:18:41.281
we want to do 1,000 to 2,000 units a year in Guelph,

02:18:41.908 --> 02:18:44.193
we don't want you to have to increase taxes for us to do it.

02:18:44.483 --> 02:18:48.233
And this is a mechanism to avoid that, subject to your finance department agreeing

02:18:48.265 --> 02:18:50.955
with my suggestion. Does that help?

02:18:53.751 --> 02:18:56.256
Oh, you're muted. Uh, you're muted, uh, Councillor Keating.

02:18:57.768 --> 02:19:01.217
Sorry, my dog is barking in the background. Uh, yeah, thank you

02:19:01.717 --> 02:19:03.554
very much. I'll follow up with staff as well. Thank you.

02:19:06.392 --> 02:19:08.958
Thank you, uh, Alt Gibson and Hauser.

02:19:10.234 --> 02:19:13.769
Uh, thank you very much, Mr. Mayor. Um, uh, through you to,

02:19:13.947 --> 02:19:17.522
uh, the delegate, Mr. LeBay. Uh, Mr. LeBay, um, I'm going to be

02:19:18.022 --> 02:19:21.374
quite clear. Uh, I am very concerned for future councils,

02:19:21.876 --> 02:19:25.051
and I have come in again like Councillor Downer, um, uh,

02:19:25.182 --> 02:19:26.838
quite enthusiastic about affordable housing,

02:19:26.854 --> 02:19:30.045
but are a number of questions that

02:19:30.061 --> 02:19:34.111
have been raised as a consequence of your, your comments

02:19:34.240 --> 02:19:38.081
and the, the previous remarks

02:19:38.195 --> 02:19:40.422
by both staff and by Mr. Handy.

02:19:42.003 --> 02:19:45.989
You mentioned that if there's a default on— there, that a person who is renting

02:19:46.102 --> 02:19:49.351
defaults on a mortgage. So the first question I have to

02:19:49.480 --> 02:19:52.052
ask you: is this a conventional mortgage?

02:19:53.794 --> 02:19:57.409
So the pre— the people have at least 2 mortgages on their titles

02:19:57.523 --> 02:20:00.996
that are that are conventional mortgage structured in a way

02:20:01.496 --> 02:20:04.522
that a mortgage is. They have the first mortgage, which is the mortgage they pay

02:20:04.586 --> 02:20:08.544
principal and interest on. The second mortgage is in favor

02:20:08.752 --> 02:20:12.245
of our system. That is a second mortgage

02:20:12.293 --> 02:20:16.059
that you don't make monthly payments on, but it has clauses that

02:20:16.559 --> 02:20:19.841
restrict your ability to sell and require you to live in the unit,

02:20:19.905 --> 02:20:23.415
or else you're in default under that mortgage. Once you're in default under

02:20:23.915 --> 02:20:27.430
a mortgage, Effectively, our organization has the right to

02:20:27.930 --> 02:20:30.822
sit down with you and ask you to remedy the default, which means move back

02:20:31.322 --> 02:20:34.439
in, or we have the ability to put you

02:20:34.440 --> 02:20:37.611
under power of sale and sell your unit and recover the money

02:20:38.111 --> 02:20:41.320
and use it for someone else. Just following up on that,

02:20:42.383 --> 02:20:45.179
if I were deciding to buy one of these units,

02:20:46.436 --> 02:20:49.779
if I opted to just go with the first mortgage because I

02:20:50.279 --> 02:20:53.384
could afford it, what's the significance of that? You would

02:20:53.884 --> 02:20:57.578
be part of the 50% that's not affordable because

02:20:57.626 --> 02:21:00.776
we've got 50% affordable. So you'd be buying one of the units that doesn't

02:21:01.276 --> 02:21:04.143
have an affordability restriction on it. So I'm going to turn the question around.

02:21:04.240 --> 02:21:07.354
Are you capping the affordable units at 50%?

02:21:09.087 --> 02:21:12.181
No, we're, we're making— if we negotiate what we

02:21:12.681 --> 02:21:16.199
intend to negotiate with staff, that's the minimum. There will

02:21:16.313 --> 02:21:19.932
be at least 20% of the units available to

02:21:20.012 --> 02:21:23.705
anybody who wants to buy without getting access to any of the affordability

02:21:24.205 --> 02:21:27.511
money. Because, as you know, a community with blended

02:21:27.559 --> 02:21:31.156
incomes is the healthiest possible community. You've got 940

02:21:31.172 --> 02:21:35.106
units, you want incomes from well-to-do down to minimum

02:21:35.606 --> 02:21:38.943
wage. And we've actually set aside 3% of these units for people on minimum wage

02:21:39.055 --> 02:21:42.476
because we think that 20-year-olds have a right to own with

02:21:42.508 --> 02:21:46.379
their first job. So there will be some of those units. 3% is a small

02:21:46.879 --> 02:21:50.186
number, but on 900 units, it's still 27

02:21:50.717 --> 02:21:54.074
20-year-olds that have never had a chance to own that will have a chance to

02:21:54.574 --> 02:21:57.882
own here. Okay. I'm going to turn it around. That's not marketing,

02:21:58.010 --> 02:22:01.561
Mr. Mayor. Just— yeah, no, I wasn't. I wasn't even thinking you

02:22:01.689 --> 02:22:05.304
were doing marketing there. I appreciate you explaining that,

02:22:05.320 --> 02:22:09.433
but I'm going to turn the question around. Are you capping the conventional units

02:22:09.933 --> 02:22:12.337
at 50%? Yes,

02:22:13.347 --> 02:22:17.259
yes, and hope not to

02:22:17.759 --> 02:22:21.603
have to get that high. So yes, we're capping how much higher income units there

02:22:22.103 --> 02:22:25.819
are because we're committing to at least the lower income affordable units.

02:22:26.316 --> 02:22:29.827
Again, I haven't retired because I want to build affordable units and change

02:22:29.843 --> 02:22:33.177
people's lives, so I have a very different motivation

02:22:33.209 --> 02:22:35.630
when I do development. Okay.

02:22:36.992 --> 02:22:40.453
Now, there's a couple of other questions. First of all, on your website—

02:22:40.953 --> 02:22:43.432
I don't know whether that's the right term anymore, but I'm going to use the

02:22:43.932 --> 02:22:46.555
word website— your units start at $150,000.

02:22:48.618 --> 02:22:51.855
So I'm wondering about that. Is that correct? And what is

02:22:52.355 --> 02:22:56.321
the size of those units, if that is correct? Very good question.

02:22:56.726 --> 02:22:57.989
That is a studio unit.

02:22:59.739 --> 02:23:03.579
That number you're looking at is related to a 400-square-foot

02:23:04.079 --> 02:23:08.584
studio in Brampton, and that unit sells for $380,000.

02:23:08.648 --> 02:23:12.050
And this is part of our problem with the provincial definition. So it sells for

02:23:12.550 --> 02:23:16.895
$380,000, but in order to get down to minimum wage, you only carry a $150,000

02:23:17.395 --> 02:23:20.794
mortgage. So the differential is lent to you and taken back when you sell.

02:23:21.419 --> 02:23:25.142
So the provincial definition should actually recognize

02:23:25.174 --> 02:23:27.805
what we call the qualifying cost price, right?

02:23:28.302 --> 02:23:32.005
But it doesn't. It just says sale price, and, and that doesn't help us with

02:23:32.505 --> 02:23:35.803
affordability. So you're right, you're seeing the mortgage size, the carrying cost

02:23:35.900 --> 02:23:39.426
size, not the purchase price. Okay, and now I just want

02:23:39.926 --> 02:23:43.754
to ask you a specific question about Home Options, not Options for Home,

02:23:44.315 --> 02:23:48.643
uh, and I was wondering if you could cite the specific projects

02:23:49.143 --> 02:23:52.586
completed. Brampton, I understand, is not completed yet. Is that correct?

02:23:53.227 --> 02:23:56.594
That's correct. It's hoping to start construction this winter. So are

02:23:57.094 --> 02:23:59.659
there any projects that Home Options has yet completed?

02:24:00.766 --> 02:24:03.622
Well, given that I created Options for Homes,

02:24:03.895 --> 02:24:07.185
we do consider that part of our portfolio. So starting

02:24:07.249 --> 02:24:10.603
way back with the first 3 buildings in the Distillery District, we're using

02:24:10.635 --> 02:24:13.732
exactly the same model and delivery mechanism.

02:24:14.149 --> 02:24:18.097
The difference is the financing is more refined and more significant

02:24:18.353 --> 02:24:22.349
to allow us to get to the incomes we're chasing. Then could you clarify

02:24:22.849 --> 02:24:25.092
more refined? Refine means more.

02:24:25.878 --> 02:24:29.568
Instead of— Options for Homes model is based on using just the deferred

02:24:30.068 --> 02:24:33.739
profits. We have now access to development charges. For the next

02:24:34.239 --> 02:24:38.374
6 months or 8 months, we have access to GST/HST. We have

02:24:38.535 --> 02:24:42.208
access to shared equity lending from Build Canada Homes.

02:24:42.481 --> 02:24:46.331
You know, we have access to many more sources of money that doesn't have

02:24:46.831 --> 02:24:49.667
to be paid back till you sell, and that allows us to reach lower incomes

02:24:49.844 --> 02:24:52.955
because people can carry a smaller portion of their home when their income is

02:24:53.051 --> 02:24:56.884
low. Yeah, thank you. I'm going to keep going because I got a number of

02:24:56.916 --> 02:25:00.828
them. You mentioned condo fees, you mentioned 20%

02:25:00.909 --> 02:25:03.987
lower, but I'm wondering if you could please provide

02:25:04.196 --> 02:25:08.060
a range of costs for condo

02:25:08.560 --> 02:25:11.235
fees. Condo fees can vary all over the place.

02:25:12.149 --> 02:25:15.821
Yeah, I'm going out on a limb here, but I feel fairly comfortable

02:25:16.321 --> 02:25:20.390
with 60 cents a square foot. $0.30 a square foot for the apartment building and

02:25:20.890 --> 02:25:24.190
$0.20 a square foot for the townhouses. So,

02:25:24.690 --> 02:25:29.010
we're looking at very specifics. So, 400 square foot would be about $280 a

02:25:29.510 --> 02:25:32.250
month. I think I just did a really, really quick math.

02:25:32.630 --> 02:25:36.050
Yeah, there are 240— 400 times 60 is 240.

02:25:36.550 --> 02:25:40.520
There you go. You're right. Yeah, my math isn't good. I've been practicing for years.

02:25:41.770 --> 02:25:46.210
Okay. Clarify for me, please, the difference between the CMHC and the provincial

02:25:46.710 --> 02:25:49.980
affordability standards. Why do you're going with CMHC?

02:25:50.639 --> 02:25:54.077
So the provincial— so first of all, the province does have

02:25:54.222 --> 02:25:57.966
other definitions, but we're referring to the definitions that's associated with the

02:25:58.190 --> 02:26:02.432
Development Charges Act. In the Development Charges Act, for a regular developer

02:26:02.932 --> 02:26:06.497
to qualify for not paying development charges in Guelph, you have

02:26:06.529 --> 02:26:10.096
to sell a unit for under $427,000,

02:26:10.707 --> 02:26:14.209
period. Doesn't matter whether it's studio or 4-bedroom, it's under $427,000.

02:26:14.819 --> 02:26:19.172
So it's not related to people's income at all. So technically,

02:26:19.766 --> 02:26:23.845
the 10% of the units, we could sell studios,

02:26:24.840 --> 02:26:27.699
10% of them, for $400,000, which qualifies,

02:26:28.197 --> 02:26:32.067
but that's also market value. Zero affordability, but it meets

02:26:32.567 --> 02:26:36.130
the definition. So the pro— my belief is

02:26:36.146 --> 02:26:39.760
the province created that definition so it wouldn't have to pay back

02:26:40.260 --> 02:26:43.195
the municipalities very much in the way of development charges. That's what it was created

02:26:43.211 --> 02:26:46.516
for. Then they put in the clause that said nonprofits don't have to pay it,

02:26:46.597 --> 02:26:50.336
period. The CMHC definition is based on 80%

02:26:51.732 --> 02:26:55.631
of the average rent by type of unit in the municipality.

02:26:56.128 --> 02:26:58.632
We take that 80% and turn it into an income.

02:26:59.514 --> 02:27:03.044
So for us now, you're looking at an income for

02:27:03.544 --> 02:27:07.216
a studio of about $45,000, and then you're looking at an income

02:27:07.716 --> 02:27:11.069
for a one-bedroom of $56,000. So the incomes go up

02:27:11.133 --> 02:27:13.413
as the size of the unit goes up, and of course, if you're dealing with

02:27:13.913 --> 02:27:17.572
a 4-bedroom with 2 incomes, $90,000, which is about the

02:27:18.072 --> 02:27:21.506
CMHC definition, is, is something that 2 incomes can do at a fairly low level.

02:27:22.004 --> 02:27:25.921
So yeah, the CMHC is more flexible, more targeted. It allows us

02:27:26.421 --> 02:27:29.839
to help families, not just single individuals in studios. Okay,

02:27:29.855 --> 02:27:33.869
I've got about 2 more questions. Um, okay, I can

02:27:34.369 --> 02:27:38.173
say You made reference to development charge and voluntarily

02:27:38.205 --> 02:27:41.866
paid after the first development. That causes

02:27:41.898 --> 02:27:45.109
me pause, and I need you to clarify that. Uh,

02:27:45.575 --> 02:27:48.898
this council is, for all intents and purposes, dissolved in

02:27:48.963 --> 02:27:52.061
3 months. So can we, uh, understand what that means?

02:27:53.137 --> 02:27:56.654
So the thought process to be worked out with your legal

02:27:57.154 --> 02:28:00.764
department and finance department, but my thought process on this is that

02:28:01.264 --> 02:28:05.017
we can't sign any kind of commitment to paying these back because the legislation

02:28:05.517 --> 02:28:08.837
says we're not supposed to pay them. So what I believe we can do,

02:28:09.126 --> 02:28:12.640
subject to testing legally, is we can sign a

02:28:13.140 --> 02:28:16.669
letter that basically says we will make best efforts to make

02:28:17.169 --> 02:28:19.606
a donation equivalent to as units resell.

02:28:20.986 --> 02:28:25.239
And so that best efforts becomes an agreement with the municipality

02:28:25.304 --> 02:28:28.525
related to the affordability that's going on, etc., etc.

02:28:28.942 --> 02:28:32.339
And so that's where my head's at, and that's what I mean. But because

02:28:32.515 --> 02:28:35.672
we're committed to doing thousands of units, we can only do

02:28:36.172 --> 02:28:39.501
that successfully without having an impact on the taxes and getting people

02:28:40.001 --> 02:28:44.052
upset with us by doing a voluntary repayment. So we actually

02:28:44.552 --> 02:28:48.891
don't need— we don't need that money after the first ownership because

02:28:48.939 --> 02:28:52.320
we're going to get more, right? We're going to just keep deferring. So that's why

02:28:52.400 --> 02:28:55.737
we can do it. But, but you're right, it's a bit of a handshake more

02:28:55.802 --> 02:28:58.251
than it is a, uh, we're going to get to whip you if you don't

02:28:58.751 --> 02:29:01.939
do it. Yeah, and that leads to my last question.

02:29:02.052 --> 02:29:06.362
It's a rotten one, I apologize, because I appreciate your vision. There's no rotten questions.

02:29:08.510 --> 02:29:11.998
What if, uh, homeownership ceases to be or

02:29:12.498 --> 02:29:15.940
can't build in Guelph because our population is capped at 207,000?

02:29:16.530 --> 02:29:18.693
That, that's a really good question. Um,

02:29:19.086 --> 02:29:22.459
First, the company that holds the

02:29:22.959 --> 02:29:25.993
mortgages is called Community Wealth Cooperative.

02:29:26.555 --> 02:29:30.875
Home Opportunities is the development consultant. Community Wealth Cooperative is our overarching

02:29:30.907 --> 02:29:34.473
financial institution that manages the

02:29:34.973 --> 02:29:38.424
money across the entire province. It's a multi-class cooperative.

02:29:38.922 --> 02:29:42.263
It historically— because I've done this once before—

02:29:42.472 --> 02:29:44.897
will survive in perpetuity. So, that's good.

02:29:45.835 --> 02:29:49.882
If Guelph reaches its cap, then the

02:29:50.382 --> 02:29:54.511
money that's committed to providing affordability in Guelph will buy existing units

02:29:54.560 --> 02:29:58.293
and make them affordable. So it'll stay within

