02:48:56.005 --> 02:48:58.999
back. So I just really want to be really clear.

02:49:00.890 --> 02:49:04.135
I, I, I'm going to give you my opinion, and then I want you

02:49:04.635 --> 02:49:10.096
to respond to this. The city has no money to

02:49:10.161 --> 02:49:14.027
be able to do multiple millions of

02:49:14.527 --> 02:49:17.411
dollars upfront, whether it's a payment or out of a reserve,

02:49:19.740 --> 02:49:23.206
to carry your

02:49:23.271 --> 02:49:27.159
structure of your project.

02:49:28.614 --> 02:49:32.672
So if there was no money from the city upfront and

02:49:32.866 --> 02:49:36.730
no payment out of any reserves, is your project

02:49:37.230 --> 02:49:40.797
still viable? For sure. Okay. For sure, because remember,

02:49:40.877 --> 02:49:44.024
we don't have to pay the development charges. Okay. Yes,

02:49:44.329 --> 02:49:46.978
I just wanted to make sure. How you deal with the fact that we don't

02:49:47.478 --> 02:49:50.816
pay them— yeah, I, I am overstepping my bounds to give you guys

02:49:50.864 --> 02:49:54.669
advice. Okay, you, you have finance people that can do

02:49:54.685 --> 02:49:58.475
that, but, but if we picture

02:49:58.975 --> 02:50:02.778
the fact that we're rolling over, so when someone sells—

02:50:03.195 --> 02:50:06.721
yep. That money comes back and we don't pay it again. Yeah, we're rolling over,

02:50:06.737 --> 02:50:10.567
so it doesn't become an accumulating pile, it becomes a pile that rolls over,

02:50:11.368 --> 02:50:14.701
for what that's worth. And again, okay, I'm going to stop commenting on finance,

02:50:14.717 --> 02:50:18.243
I'm going to get myself into trouble. I— but you're absolutely

02:50:18.259 --> 02:50:21.672
correct, Mayor. How you deal with

02:50:21.720 --> 02:50:24.412
the fact we don't have to pay development charges is your problem, not ours.

02:50:25.085 --> 02:50:28.547
Correct, because of the provincial legislation. Exactly. I know, and I appreciate that.

02:50:28.627 --> 02:50:32.106
That's very clear. So, so you're

02:50:32.606 --> 02:50:36.594
being nice is what is where I come down to it. The provincial legislation says

02:50:36.772 --> 02:50:40.480
you don't have to pay, and if you— if we

02:50:40.980 --> 02:50:43.790
agreed with this project, you could just not pay.

02:50:44.272 --> 02:50:48.096
But what you're saying at the microphone today, for us as the decision makers

02:50:48.674 --> 02:50:52.004
and for the city, is even though we don't have to pay, we have a

02:50:52.504 --> 02:50:55.504
mechanism to actually pay you back over time in the future. Future.

02:50:55.907 --> 02:51:00.006
And I believe that to be a smart strategic decision by us around

02:51:00.506 --> 02:51:03.572
our model. Okay, all right. Um,

02:51:03.717 --> 02:51:07.290
and now I want to get back to Councillor Bucetil

02:51:08.222 --> 02:51:12.435
and Councillor Downer's question, because for

02:51:12.935 --> 02:51:17.469
the love of everything, I wanted to come in here and

02:51:17.485 --> 02:51:21.432
get to yes. Okay, in fact A lot

02:51:21.932 --> 02:51:25.697
of us do, okay? But I

02:51:26.051 --> 02:51:29.442
actually agree with you that it would be

02:51:29.506 --> 02:51:32.929
great if this term of council got to yes.

02:51:36.075 --> 02:51:39.479
So staff's going to kill me with what I'm going to say.

02:51:42.143 --> 02:51:45.678
Plug your ears, staff. If we— we have a council

02:51:46.178 --> 02:51:49.448
meeting next week. Is it

02:51:49.948 --> 02:51:54.163
better to hear you out tonight about

02:51:54.663 --> 02:51:59.888
what is doable on the alternative motions and/or

02:52:00.497 --> 02:52:04.378
not doable on the alternative motions and to

02:52:04.442 --> 02:52:08.131
take that feedback from us and let us

02:52:08.355 --> 02:52:12.140
defer the decision as you try to work a little

02:52:12.640 --> 02:52:16.518
bit more with staff and come back

02:52:17.018 --> 02:52:18.274
with a more solid position,

02:52:20.370 --> 02:52:24.325
potentially, I can't, you know, no crystal ball, but potentially get

02:52:24.825 --> 02:52:28.411
to a better yes than an

02:52:28.753 --> 02:52:31.785
unreasonable, than a yes that doesn't help you.

02:52:34.492 --> 02:52:37.975
Because Councillor Downar is right. Like, when we make

02:52:37.991 --> 02:52:41.720
a decision here, Okay? It, like,

02:52:41.963 --> 02:52:44.893
the parameters embedded within the alternative motions,

02:52:45.751 --> 02:52:49.409
like, that's it. Council as a

02:52:49.909 --> 02:52:52.928
body has made a decision and has directed staff.

02:52:54.720 --> 02:52:58.109
That's it. So if you don't like 1, 2, 3,

02:52:58.190 --> 02:53:02.257
4, or 5 things about that decision and

02:53:02.289 --> 02:53:04.358
it makes your project not viable,

02:53:05.608 --> 02:53:06.559
Nobody wins.

02:53:09.076 --> 02:53:13.012
So I am concerned from

02:53:13.060 --> 02:53:16.898
a position of trying to get to yes, if you need

02:53:17.398 --> 02:53:21.011
more time. And so I'm just wondering, like,

02:53:22.108 --> 02:53:25.271
if we go through another hour and a bit of delegations and then

02:53:25.771 --> 02:53:28.950
we have some negotiation, you know, discussion amongst council about the

02:53:29.450 --> 02:53:32.602
alternative motions, And let's say we land on whatever

02:53:33.102 --> 02:53:37.140
the decision of council is to move forward on this, but it doesn't meet

02:53:37.640 --> 02:53:40.777
what you need to be viable. Then,

02:53:40.891 --> 02:53:43.048
like, no one's high-fiving each other on the way out the door.

02:53:44.212 --> 02:53:47.593
So would it be prudent? Do you feel like

02:53:48.093 --> 02:53:52.440
it would be prudent if we start moving towards a decision tonight

02:53:52.504 --> 02:53:56.129
that's not going to make your project viable, that it would be better

02:53:56.227 --> 02:53:59.910
off for us as a council to say, we hear you,

02:54:01.790 --> 02:54:05.492
we would prefer you defer this decision as we work with staff and

02:54:05.558 --> 02:54:09.146
come back at another time. I'm just— I'm really

02:54:09.646 --> 02:54:12.928
trying to weigh this out here a little bit to the benefit of what you're

02:54:13.428 --> 02:54:16.468
trying to do. So I'll stop there. How do you respond?

02:54:20.141 --> 02:54:23.478
Ideally for me, I would have 60 seconds to

02:54:23.978 --> 02:54:27.783
