02:54:28.506 --> 02:54:32.650
If I don't have the 60 seconds to confer, I would be

02:54:33.150 --> 02:54:36.907
quite open to a motion that approves the change of use.

02:54:37.405 --> 02:54:39.862
Sure. So that that's done, taken care of.

02:54:41.163 --> 02:54:44.456
The flexibility and innovation that staff has shown to

02:54:44.956 --> 02:54:48.215
date tells me that they are willing to sit down and

02:54:48.311 --> 02:54:51.605
talk things through with us has to come up with something that works for everybody,

02:54:51.621 --> 02:54:54.804
including the city. And so

02:54:54.998 --> 02:54:58.686
by approving the change of use and saying come back

02:54:58.944 --> 02:55:02.052
with a discussion as to the amendments you suggested to

02:55:02.068 --> 02:55:05.413
the alternative, we might come back in

02:55:05.495 --> 02:55:09.186
front of you with something closer. Okay, so let's

02:55:09.686 --> 02:55:13.098
park that. I'm going to give you more than 60 seconds because I think it's

02:55:13.147 --> 02:55:16.927
still relevant for us to move through the delegations to

02:55:17.427 --> 02:55:20.716
hear more, to discuss, for us to reengage with our

02:55:21.216 --> 02:55:24.634
staff, maybe even take a break for 5 minutes. And we

02:55:25.134 --> 02:55:28.729
can catch our breath and go from there. Because I'm telling you,

02:55:29.114 --> 02:55:32.502
I believe, I might be wrong, I believe

02:55:33.002 --> 02:55:36.645
there's an atmosphere of trying to get to yes. But I don't

02:55:36.741 --> 02:55:40.145
want the yes to make your project not

02:55:40.645 --> 02:55:44.190
viable. So please, please know I'm coming from a good space

02:55:44.626 --> 02:55:48.191
as the chair, as the mayor, trying to make sure that we're

02:55:48.691 --> 02:55:52.071
trying to move in a direction that puts shovels in the ground and gives affordability

02:55:52.571 --> 02:55:56.359
to people. So with that being said, I was the last person to ask questions

02:55:56.859 --> 02:56:00.088
and talk. So thank you. We'll go on to the next. We'll continue to hear

02:56:00.588 --> 02:56:05.129
and engage as we move forward with the delegations. And then let's

02:56:05.629 --> 02:56:09.432
get through the delegations. If we can, everybody. And then we

02:56:09.932 --> 02:56:13.125
will engage with our staff, and then after that, we'll just have a quick break,

02:56:13.141 --> 02:56:16.738
okay? So thank you again, Mike. We appreciate

02:56:17.238 --> 02:56:20.929
it. I appreciate it as well. Uh, Tywin Thomas from Urban Metrics

02:56:21.057 --> 02:56:24.076
is up next, and then is Lucas Arnold here?

02:56:24.670 --> 02:56:25.826
Okay, you're in the wings.

02:56:37.520 --> 02:56:49.278
I'm so sorry,

02:56:54.560 --> 02:56:59.206
but time has not started. It only starts when you Good

02:56:59.706 --> 02:57:02.654
evening, Mayor Guthrie and Council, staff, and members of the community.

02:57:03.279 --> 02:57:06.535
My name is Tywin Thomas. I'm an associate partner with Urban Metrics.

02:57:06.823 --> 02:57:10.993
We were retained to carry out an employment land assessment and an economic benefits

02:57:11.493 --> 02:57:14.682
assessment. In this context, I'm here to discuss kind of the key

02:57:15.182 --> 02:57:18.771
findings of our work and then hopefully, you know, answer any questions that

02:57:19.271 --> 02:57:22.460
anyone has at the end. So I'll kind of begin with the first

02:57:22.960 --> 02:57:25.282
thing that we always to look at, which is looking at the site itself and

02:57:25.782 --> 02:57:29.580
its characteristics. So, you know, it's been kind of discussed at length already that

02:57:30.080 --> 02:57:34.071
there's, there's an ecological linkage, there's a variety of slopes, there's limited frontage,

02:57:34.632 --> 02:57:38.064
and there's an irregular shape that are all contributing to constraining

02:57:38.657 --> 02:57:42.218
the potential to build kind of a stereotypical industrial

02:57:42.330 --> 02:57:45.554
development. Um, you know, we looked at kind

02:57:46.054 --> 02:57:49.612
of market dynamics throughout Guelph and throughout the region, and The property

02:57:49.628 --> 02:57:53.208
was fully zoned for industrial uses throughout much of a cycle

02:57:53.273 --> 02:57:56.629
of elevated industrial development in Guelph and

02:57:56.853 --> 02:58:00.450
in the surrounding area, and it remained vacant throughout this period.

02:58:01.285 --> 02:58:04.416
You know, we look at the surrounding uses, and as such, we consider that a

02:58:04.480 --> 02:58:07.932
probable use is a lighter intensity logistics and

02:58:08.432 --> 02:58:10.967
transportation use like the neighboring Roadship Freight Systems,

02:58:11.963 --> 02:58:15.305
and we found little market basis to support a conclusion

02:58:15.337 --> 02:58:18.960
that, that a more intensive industrial development that generates

02:58:19.460 --> 02:58:22.712
more employment and economic activity than that is something that

02:58:22.728 --> 02:58:25.050
would be likely to occur on the property.

02:58:26.455 --> 02:58:29.943
We also looked at kind of the employment lands arithmetic,

02:58:30.734 --> 02:58:35.690
and the main kind of consideration that we evaluated is

02:58:35.869 --> 02:58:39.348
that with the introduction of the PPS 2024, the types

02:58:39.397 --> 02:58:43.015
of employment that are permitted within employment areas changed

02:58:43.031 --> 02:58:46.692
and the number of jobs that are therefore allocated to employment areas was

02:58:46.805 --> 02:58:50.498
reduced. And that means that you need slightly less land than was

02:58:50.578 --> 02:58:53.869
previously anticipated for employment area uses and you

02:58:54.369 --> 02:58:57.562
need to plan for more jobs in other policy areas throughout the city.

02:58:58.590 --> 02:59:02.347
And so based on these calculations, this was quite a considerable amount of

02:59:02.363 --> 02:59:07.646
jobs in Guelph's case that were major office or population

02:59:08.146 --> 02:59:11.745
related employment. So based on our assessment, after subtracting these jobs that were previously allocated

02:59:11.777 --> 02:59:16.646
to employment areas, the city is in a position of retaining a surplus relative

02:59:16.678 --> 02:59:20.213
to projected industrial employment. This was kind

02:59:20.713 --> 02:59:24.391
of helped by the fact that the actual developable area of the site that's factored

02:59:24.891 --> 02:59:28.778
into the land supply is relatively limited by, again, the ecological linkage

02:59:28.826 --> 02:59:32.959
and some other factors. And so it only represented about about 1.5%

02:59:33.152 --> 02:59:36.839
of the city's inventory at the time of the last employment land

02:59:37.339 --> 02:59:40.960
strategy. And I understand that that's been both absorbed and converted and been reduced

02:59:41.460 --> 02:59:44.933
since then as well. And again, our conclusion was that

02:59:45.045 --> 02:59:49.066
even in this case, there was sufficient land to accommodate projected industrial

02:59:49.566 --> 02:59:52.253
employment. On the opposite side,

02:59:59.320 --> 03:00:03.221
Thanks. Thanks. Conversion creates the potential for a series

03:00:03.253 --> 03:00:06.272
of economic benefits. It would, as it's been mentioned,

03:00:06.722 --> 03:00:10.399
it would deliver hundreds of homes within the financial and the geographic reach of

03:00:10.899 --> 03:00:14.349
local employees and families. And it would create construction employment

03:00:15.055 --> 03:00:18.604
using both nonprofit analysis as

03:00:18.652 --> 03:00:22.248
well as looking at the input/output tables that Statistics Canada puts forward

03:00:22.264 --> 03:00:26.196
for construction. Construction investment, potentially 500 to 700 jobs

03:00:26.696 --> 03:00:30.513
in construction, and involving local, local modular fabrication.

03:00:31.332 --> 03:00:35.167
Um, in addition to kind of these employment benefits, annual property

03:00:35.199 --> 03:00:38.505
taxes are significantly higher, approximately 10 times based on our

03:00:39.005 --> 03:00:43.432
modeling, than a typical industrial development would generate. So beyond

03:00:43.464 --> 03:00:47.524
those quantifiable benefits, there's, there's the social benefits of delivering

03:00:47.556 --> 03:00:51.321
housing that's attainable for a large portion of of the local workforce, and there's a

03:00:51.498 --> 03:00:55.435
kind of growing body of research that this has pretty significant employee

03:00:55.935 --> 03:00:59.210
retention benefits for local employers, and I believe that there's some employers here to

03:00:59.710 --> 03:01:03.683
speak to that today. So kind of to conclude from

03:01:03.747 --> 03:01:07.234
a more policy-driven perspective, the PPS 2024

03:01:07.298 --> 03:01:10.496
sets out 4 tests for an employment area conversion or removal,

03:01:10.897 --> 03:01:13.747
and 2 of these tests are addressed within our scope of work,

03:01:14.161 --> 03:01:17.685
and the City of The Gulf Official Plan has similar tests as well,

03:01:18.378 --> 03:01:21.760
that there's a need for the conversion and the land is not required for employment

03:01:21.776 --> 03:01:25.894
purposes and that the municipality retains sufficient land to accommodate

03:01:26.394 --> 03:01:29.591
projected employment growth. So we believe that both of those

03:01:29.688 --> 03:01:31.698
things are true and we believe that that's been demonstrated.

03:01:33.577 --> 03:01:37.547
To summarize, the site has constraints that limit its development potential and a higher

03:01:38.047 --> 03:01:41.741
intensity use is needed to overcome these limitations. Much-needed affordable

03:01:42.241 --> 03:01:46.148
and market-rate housing is a proposed solution. Um, we find that the city would retain

03:01:46.197 --> 03:01:49.381
a sufficient supply of employment land and

03:01:49.397 --> 03:01:53.030
that the economic and fiscal benefits that could be realized through the conversion are

03:01:53.530 --> 03:01:56.749
significant. Um, with that, I thank you for your time. I'm happy to answer any

03:01:57.249 --> 03:01:58.463
questions from council on our work.

03:02:00.974 --> 03:02:04.142
Great, thank you. Wrapped it right up right before, right before your

03:02:04.642 --> 03:02:08.357
5 minutes. So thank you, um, and thank you for your presentation and the charts

03:02:08.857 --> 03:02:12.419
and stuff. We appreciate that. Is there any follow-up on this delegation

03:02:12.467 --> 03:02:16.208
at this time? Councillor Gibson. Thank you, and I'm sorry, I didn't— I haven't

03:02:16.708 --> 03:02:19.885
read through your complete economic analysis, but I just wondered how granular it gets.

03:02:20.479 --> 03:02:25.826
Do you get down to just even the thought of 1,000

03:02:25.858 --> 03:02:29.551
people living next door to potential employment and the ability

03:02:30.051 --> 03:02:32.297
to move around the city and the ease at which people would move around the

03:02:32.797 --> 03:02:35.295
city I'm sure they don't— most of them would have cars, but they wouldn't need

03:02:35.795 --> 03:02:38.113
to take them to work, right, if they worked in the area. I get— did

03:02:38.613 --> 03:02:42.042
it look at— did you— did your economic analysis look at the benefits of that

03:02:42.542 --> 03:02:46.138
proximity to employment from an ease of navigation and an

03:02:46.315 --> 03:02:48.992
ease of services off of the rest of us, like, say,

03:02:49.234 --> 03:02:52.698
transit and things like that? I would— through you,

03:02:52.988 --> 03:02:56.274
Mr. Mayor, I would say that the granularity was really trying to

03:02:56.371 --> 03:02:59.490
focus on looking at the two-digit NAICS code level of employment.

03:02:59.618 --> 03:03:02.918
It's really hard to kind of quantify some of those benefits

03:03:03.623 --> 03:03:06.892
of being able to live so close to work. I mean,

03:03:06.924 --> 03:03:10.818
it's something that, you know, people strive for to kind of minimize

03:03:10.850 --> 03:03:13.990
their commute as much as possible. And so I'm sure from my perspective,

03:03:14.038 --> 03:03:17.627
that's a benefit, but very difficult to quantify. And we didn't kind of

03:03:17.964 --> 03:03:21.601
get to it beyond more of a literature review of the body of

03:03:21.649 --> 03:03:25.030
work that suggests that providing housing in the same community

03:03:25.046 --> 03:03:28.761
as where you work is beneficial. Beneficial to both the employee and their family,

03:03:28.843 --> 03:03:32.747
but also the employer. Great. And maybe a question

03:03:33.247 --> 03:03:36.022
for you or someone else, just the sheer walkability of the area as well,

03:03:36.135 --> 03:03:39.666
being so close to services, being so close to all that commercial amenities

03:03:39.699 --> 03:03:43.563
just up the road at Clare and Gordon. Did that factor into your economic analysis,

03:03:43.579 --> 03:03:46.959
or is that somebody else's— that's somebody else's report? Am I stealing someone else's script?

03:03:47.525 --> 03:03:50.478
There, there may be some that can, that can speak more directly to that,

03:03:50.561 --> 03:03:54.111
but no, it wasn't something that we looked at specifically from an economic perspective,

03:03:54.192 --> 03:03:57.724
but it was certainly discussed in the planning report and others.

03:03:59.070 --> 03:04:02.873
Thank you. Well, thank you. I don't see any more questions.

03:04:03.002 --> 03:04:07.613
So thank you very much. We appreciate it. And Lucas

03:04:07.889 --> 03:04:11.258
Arnold from Dillon is here. And then is Sal Spital

03:04:11.517 --> 03:04:14.562
here? Did I say your last name right? I did. You're in the wings.

03:04:17.292 --> 03:04:20.518
Lucas, uh, thank you so much for waiting around and thanks for joining us.

03:04:20.598 --> 03:04:24.257
I'll turn it over to you. Thank you. Uh, good evening, Mayor Guthrie

03:04:24.757 --> 03:04:27.852
and members of council. Uh, my name is Lucas Arnold. I'm a professional engineer with

03:04:27.884 --> 03:04:30.484
Dillon Consulting and a local partner in our Guelph office.

03:04:31.158 --> 03:04:34.481
My professional practice focuses on environmental noise and

03:04:34.545 --> 03:04:37.594
industrial land use compatibility. Our objective

03:04:38.094 --> 03:04:40.676
through this process has been twofold. Uh, one,

03:04:40.949 --> 03:04:45.073
to establish a suitable environment for

03:04:45.573 --> 03:04:49.153
future residents, and two, protecting the ongoing operational viability and regulatory compliance of

03:04:49.653 --> 03:04:53.392
the existing industrial facilities in the area. Following our initial studies in

03:04:53.892 --> 03:04:57.679
October 2024, we reviewed the technical comments issued by City Planning staff

03:04:57.887 --> 03:05:01.195
and the city's peer reviewer, uh, RJ Burnside and Associates.

03:05:01.982 --> 03:05:05.964
We updated our air quality and detailed noise studies in March of 2026

03:05:06.012 --> 03:05:09.322
to address all comments and continue to demonstrate alignment

03:05:09.822 --> 03:05:13.484
with the PPS, MECP guidelines, and the City of Guelph

03:05:13.581 --> 03:05:17.325
noise control guidelines. To conduct these evaluations,

03:05:17.438 --> 03:05:20.985
we utilized source-specific operational data. We acquired

03:05:21.485 --> 03:05:25.173
the 2011 air quality and acoustic reports for the TDL facility

03:05:26.088 --> 03:05:29.510
directly from the facility operators. For Denso Manufacturing,

03:05:29.703 --> 03:05:32.996
we obtained their 2020— sorry, 2016 reports through a

03:05:33.496 --> 03:05:37.226
Freedom of Information request along with their 2023

03:05:37.679 --> 03:05:41.255
emission summary tables. Through the process, we,

03:05:41.401 --> 03:05:45.073
we proactively engaged the adjacent industries starting in early 2024

03:05:45.364 --> 03:05:48.664
to request details on their current operations and

03:05:48.987 --> 03:05:52.158
any planned future expansions. Despite our,

03:05:52.222 --> 03:05:55.681
you know, repeated efforts, no specific expansion plans have

03:05:55.697 --> 03:05:58.333
been provided for us for an inclusion in our assessments.

03:05:59.137 --> 03:06:02.655
Our predictive modeling relies on the objective data available through

03:06:03.204 --> 03:06:05.272
official channels and engineering best practice.

03:06:07.050 --> 03:06:09.905
Green button. Wrong one.

03:06:11.422 --> 03:06:14.713
There it is. As shown

03:06:15.213 --> 03:06:18.387
on the screen, the site layout provides spatial separation from the

03:06:18.887 --> 03:06:23.480
adjacent properties. The ecological linkage and proposed roadway position

03:06:23.980 --> 03:06:27.243
the sensitive uses outside of the MBCP D Series guidelines

03:06:27.516 --> 03:06:30.987
recommended minimum separation distance. Based on

03:06:31.487 --> 03:06:34.924
the information we were able to obtain, the air dis— air dispersion

03:06:34.972 --> 03:06:38.620
modeling from our air quality team shows that

03:06:39.120 --> 03:06:43.247
the maximum point of impingement concentration for the regulated contaminants

03:06:43.747 --> 03:06:47.200
remain well below the MECP criteria, including at the elevated

03:06:47.700 --> 03:06:51.378
receptors. Additionally, nuisance impacts from fugitive dust

03:06:51.442 --> 03:06:53.434
and odor are not expected. Expected at the proposed development.

03:06:54.510 --> 03:06:58.042
From an acoustic perspective, we are recommending a Class 4 area designation for

03:06:58.542 --> 03:07:02.056
the proposed development. Under provincial guidance, this is a recognized

03:07:02.556 --> 03:07:05.829
planning tool intended for the redevelopment and

03:07:05.894 --> 03:07:09.940
infill for development prox— in proximity to existing stationary

03:07:10.440 --> 03:07:13.793
sources. The designation provides a framework that

03:07:14.293 --> 03:07:18.096
achieves acoustic compatibility for the future residents while providing the surrounding industry

03:07:18.596 --> 03:07:21.002
industries with long-term operational flexibility.

03:07:21.725 --> 03:07:25.000
The city's independent peer reviewer supported the use of Class 4

03:07:25.500 --> 03:07:29.304
for this area. Based on the updated studies, the proposed

03:07:29.804 --> 03:07:33.494
development is not anticipated to limit the ability of the surrounding industries

03:07:34.249 --> 03:07:37.557
to operate in compliance with their ECAs, their environmental compliance approvals.

03:07:38.279 --> 03:07:42.117
It's expected that the proposed development can coexist compatibly with

03:07:42.617 --> 03:07:45.959
the surrounding area. Thank you, and I welcome any technical

03:07:46.459 --> 03:07:49.336
questions. Great, thank you very much.

03:07:49.853 --> 03:07:53.581
Any questions around the land use compatibility study here from them?

03:07:53.662 --> 03:07:56.550
Okay, go ahead. Sorry, I didn't mean to be all me tonight.

03:07:57.410 --> 03:08:00.627
You're my sound guy though. Yeah, that's me. Through you, Mr. Mayor,

03:08:01.791 --> 03:08:05.688
I— in Ward 1, we've got a landing strip and we've got scrap

03:08:06.188 --> 03:08:08.883
metal and we've got some industrial processes. Very familiar.

03:08:09.156 --> 03:08:12.272
And I hear from residents all the time, these noises, these noises, and I have

03:08:12.288 --> 03:08:15.725
to remind them gently that those existed before the houses. So it's

03:08:15.757 --> 03:08:18.937
a hard, it's a hard one to find. But in your instance, you'll be putting

03:08:18.953 --> 03:08:22.760
lots of houses next to industrial processes, so the complaints will be the same.

03:08:23.531 --> 03:08:26.807
How do you plan to mitigate those concerns from residents?

03:08:27.064 --> 03:08:30.148
I have ideas, but I'm sure I'll let you go first. So that's a great

03:08:30.648 --> 03:08:33.232
question. Through you, Your Worship, so our study,

03:08:34.452 --> 03:08:37.404
our study is based on ministry best practice and engineering best practice.

03:08:38.383 --> 03:08:41.686
And the limits that we're assessing are, are set based on adverse

03:08:42.186 --> 03:08:45.616
effects, which generally produce complaints. So we're not predicted

03:08:46.116 --> 03:08:49.497
to see many adverse effects. Now, the Class 4 is being introduced, which, which does

03:08:49.609 --> 03:08:52.945
increase the sound levels. With the Class 4, there are some— there are

03:08:52.993 --> 03:08:56.794
additional warning clauses on title that are pretty strict, as well

03:08:56.842 --> 03:09:00.161
as required air conditioning for those times when it is loud in

03:09:00.193 --> 03:09:03.452
the area that residents are able to shut their windows. They're supplied

03:09:03.485 --> 03:09:06.815
with air conditioning. Okay, great. So I

03:09:06.847 --> 03:09:09.595
was— first question I was going to ask was, are you going to create balconies

03:09:09.627 --> 03:09:12.946
on the side where all the sound is? And I'm hoping you're going to say

03:09:13.446 --> 03:09:15.870
no, we're not going to do balconies on the— on the— would it be the

03:09:16.370 --> 03:09:19.990
southwest side? So I believe— I believe there— there could

03:09:20.490 --> 03:09:24.479
be balconies on the west side. So the regulatory compliance—

03:09:24.593 --> 03:09:28.120
4 or 5 councillors are going to be busy. With the regulatory compliance and

03:09:29.762 --> 03:09:33.161
the industries are required to meet at what's defined as a sensitive receptor by the

03:09:33.661 --> 03:09:37.810
Ministry of the Environment. Balconies that are not—

03:09:37.955 --> 03:09:41.578
that are less deep than 4 meters are not considered a sensitive use where industries

03:09:42.078 --> 03:09:45.332
are not required to meet. So yes, there may be, if an individual is

03:09:45.832 --> 03:09:47.872
out there, there may be, but they'll have the ability to go inside if it's

03:09:47.937 --> 03:09:51.738
too loud. Okay. And then I've also heard of situations where

03:09:52.238 --> 03:09:55.652
they'll— They'll have those facing— the windows

03:09:55.764 --> 03:09:58.703
facing that noise receptor not being windows that open,

03:09:59.056 --> 03:10:02.588
basically. So that's

03:10:03.088 --> 03:10:06.378
been used before, but that's not a proper form for mitigation. Okay. The noise criteria

03:10:06.878 --> 03:10:09.204
is actually on the outside of the window, allowing the resident to keep it open,

03:10:09.252 --> 03:10:12.865
where industries are obligated to meet legally through the Ministry of the Environment. So sealed

03:10:13.365 --> 03:10:16.847
windows are not an acceptable form for residential use. They are for schools,

03:10:16.943 --> 03:10:20.327
hospitals, daycare in institutional uses, but not for residential. Okay,

03:10:21.224 --> 03:10:24.415
I'm deep enough in the weeds. I'm glad that it's on your mind. Thank you.

03:10:24.832 --> 03:10:28.455
All right, thank you, Councillor Gibson. I don't see anything further on this

03:10:28.583 --> 03:10:32.158
one, so again, thank you very much for joining us. Thank you. Oh, I'm sorry,

03:10:32.575 --> 03:10:36.005
I was incorrect. Councillor Colar has a question. Um,

03:10:36.454 --> 03:10:40.302
thank you, Your Worship. I'm just hoping to get a little bit of clarity about

03:10:40.366 --> 03:10:44.397
the level of noise. I've got some of my constituents who live

03:10:44.897 --> 03:10:47.383
on Empire Street, you know, they back onto a PDA plant,

03:10:48.012 --> 03:10:51.644
and when those— they're loading the train

03:10:52.144 --> 03:10:55.585
