A, not being aware of wildlife, and wildlife not being aware

03:18:48.646 --> 03:18:52.416
of cars because they're not. Yes. Yeah, through you,

03:18:52.432 --> 03:18:56.283
Mr. Mayor, that's a good question. And I think that goes with

03:18:56.412 --> 03:18:59.189
whether there's a curve or not. Wildlife.

03:19:00.121 --> 03:19:03.591
They're unfortunately unable to read signs and/or

03:19:03.656 --> 03:19:07.045
understand how crossings work. There's two ways to mitigate this.

03:19:07.174 --> 03:19:10.467
One, for small mammals, there's a recommendation for a

03:19:10.967 --> 03:19:14.323
wildlife crossing to be designed and incorporated

03:19:14.823 --> 03:19:18.356
if there's any future roadworks. There's a recommendation

03:19:18.856 --> 03:19:22.324
for that that would accommodate wildlife movement for small wildlife.

03:19:22.774 --> 03:19:26.513
For larger wildlife, unfortunately The— there's not a lot— you're

03:19:27.013 --> 03:19:29.719
not going to design an overpass. That doesn't happen.

03:19:30.633 --> 03:19:33.407
What we typically recommend is signage, flashing lights,

03:19:34.304 --> 03:19:38.088
ways to alert drivers to the

03:19:38.588 --> 03:19:42.208
potential for wildlife crossing in that area. So that's used in other

03:19:42.641 --> 03:19:45.783
jurisdictions. I'm not sure, but I believe the City

03:19:46.283 --> 03:19:50.143
of Guelph's wildlife crossing designs also make those recommendations for

03:19:50.755 --> 03:19:54.576
ways to mitigate impacts, including collisions with wildlife

03:19:54.786 --> 03:19:57.656
where there is a higher chance of wildlife crossing roads.

03:20:02.740 --> 03:20:04.653
I see a thumbs up. Thank you, thank you. Welcome.

03:20:06.125 --> 03:20:06.828
And Hauser, please.

03:20:09.736 --> 03:20:12.380
Um, thank you for the presentation. And through the mayor, I know,

03:20:13.122 --> 03:20:16.604
um, we have some endangered species that are in there. You touched on a

03:20:17.104 --> 03:20:21.006
little bit of what might be done to mitigate their, their moving.

03:20:21.102 --> 03:20:24.326
So their trees are going to be cut down, like, for bats. And then we

03:20:24.826 --> 03:20:28.224
have an Eastern Meadowlark, which is a ground bird. What is going

03:20:28.240 --> 03:20:31.624
to be done, like, if— can

03:20:32.124 --> 03:20:36.291
they nest in, like, a flood area that's going to be set up for

03:20:36.371 --> 03:20:39.916
storm management? I'm just confused. How would, how would we

03:20:39.948 --> 03:20:43.284
mitigate their movement? Yeah, good question. Thank you.

03:20:43.573 --> 03:20:46.957
Through you, Mr. Mayor, so we don't actually expect that there are

03:20:47.457 --> 03:20:51.131
any Eastern Meadowlark breeding on the site right now. It doesn't have the right configuration

03:20:51.631 --> 03:20:54.744
or size to support breeding habitat for Eastern Meadowlark.

03:20:54.808 --> 03:20:59.288
It's not to say that they're not using it as adjacent area for

03:20:59.304 --> 03:21:02.499
foraging and/or doesn't contribute to their primary habitat.

03:21:03.061 --> 03:21:06.658
Based on guidance and what we know of

03:21:06.690 --> 03:21:10.479
their habitat preferences, that would be located south of the

03:21:10.979 --> 03:21:14.237
subject lands in terms of the— it has the right configuration and size where you

03:21:14.737 --> 03:21:17.833
might expect Eastern Meadowlark to breed. Yes, they breed on the ground,

03:21:17.849 --> 03:21:21.976
but I wouldn't expect them currently to be breeding on the subject lands and/or

03:21:22.088 --> 03:21:24.593
in the future. It just doesn't have the right configuration,

03:21:25.219 --> 03:21:28.688
uh, dimensions for them to breed there. They're technically considered

03:21:28.848 --> 03:21:32.333
area sensitive, and the area is not there in terms of the configuration on

03:21:32.381 --> 03:21:36.347
subject lands. Um, with respect to bats,

03:21:36.620 --> 03:21:40.088
uh, yes, there will be a need to replace habitat.

03:21:40.971 --> 03:21:44.375
And there are— you've probably seen or heard of rocket boxes or bat

03:21:44.487 --> 03:21:48.084
condos. That's a typical form, an accepted form of

03:21:48.100 --> 03:21:51.488
mitigation, and it's considered a short-term

03:21:51.809 --> 03:21:55.326
form of mitigation. The long-term form of mitigation is

03:21:55.826 --> 03:21:58.360
actually the planting of the trees. Bats prefer trees,

03:21:59.661 --> 03:22:02.920
that's where you mostly find them. But in the meantime, yes, there will be

03:22:04.958 --> 03:22:08.153
bat boxes placed within the ecological linkage

03:22:08.492 --> 03:22:11.897
until such time that those trees mature and then those that

03:22:12.397 --> 03:22:14.064
they themselves provide the habitat for bats.

03:22:17.713 --> 03:22:20.582
Thank you very much. You're welcome. All right,

03:22:20.631 --> 03:22:24.125
thank you. Um, Councillor Chu

03:22:24.403 --> 03:22:26.089
and then Councillor Caron.

03:22:30.168 --> 03:22:34.022
Thank you. Through you, Mr. Mayor, thank you for your presentation. I just

03:22:34.522 --> 03:22:38.598
want to— just help me understand the numbers of trees being planted. I think about

03:22:39.098 --> 03:22:42.371
50 or so invasive buckthorn is being removed and

03:22:42.387 --> 03:22:45.856
about 300 or so as well, um,

03:22:46.723 --> 03:22:51.748
and there's going to be about 1,350 native species being replanted.

03:22:52.278 --> 03:22:55.490
So can you help me understand within that 60 meter

03:22:55.634 --> 03:22:59.777
buffer, like how you're going to be able to accommodate for some of those saplings

03:23:00.435 --> 03:23:04.128
and how that's actually going to be beneficial for the environment here that

03:23:04.628 --> 03:23:07.050
we're trying to create. Thank you, through you, Mr.

03:23:07.099 --> 03:23:11.016
Mayor. So the area of

03:23:11.370 --> 03:23:14.420
the ecological linkage can accommodate

03:23:14.742 --> 03:23:18.531
that many trees and shrubs at a density which is typically recommended

03:23:19.157 --> 03:23:22.465
for planting to ensure proper spacing, and so on.

03:23:22.529 --> 03:23:25.692
So that— we've already looked at the area that's available,

03:23:26.045 --> 03:23:29.642
and our recommendations for densities and species is

03:23:29.658 --> 03:23:33.191
based on that. So we'll have a much higher number

03:23:33.207 --> 03:23:36.659
of trees planted within Ecological Linkage that are currently on

03:23:37.159 --> 03:23:40.866
site. And yes, any, any buckthorn, any invasive species that are currently there

03:23:41.379 --> 03:23:44.928
will need to be removed and treated, and there will need

03:23:45.428 --> 03:23:49.231
to be a monitoring management plan even within the restored ecological linkage

03:23:49.344 --> 03:23:52.121
to ensure that invasive species don't come in,

03:23:52.234 --> 03:23:56.168
including buckthorn. So that will be a part of it, but the end result will

03:23:56.668 --> 03:23:59.828
be a much higher number and density of trees, shrubs,

03:24:00.262 --> 03:24:03.489
and other native vegetation, also with a higher diversity.

03:24:03.570 --> 03:24:07.471
So overall, but the biodiversity within the ecological

03:24:07.971 --> 03:24:12.668
linkage and its function will be higher than is currently found on the subject plants.

03:24:12.749 --> 03:24:16.631
Okay, thank you. And through you, Mr. May, one more question. So I

03:24:17.131 --> 03:24:20.965
attend the annual tree planting every year, just adjacent

03:24:21.465 --> 03:24:24.672
to the site, uh, that's done by TreeFires for the Well. Can you help me

03:24:24.688 --> 03:24:28.476
understand, like, how the— how you're planning to partner with different

03:24:28.976 --> 03:24:32.661
community groups to help, uh, regenerate some of the soil health from

03:24:33.161 --> 03:24:36.556
all this invasive buckthorn that's pretty rampant and invasive? And also,

03:24:36.621 --> 03:24:40.202
how do we protect some of the biodiversity that's going to

03:24:40.702 --> 03:24:44.987
be brought forward into the site with all this native trees planting?

03:24:45.806 --> 03:24:48.953
Because I think that's probably the biggest question I get when I go to these

03:24:49.453 --> 03:24:52.951
events. How do these saplings survive with all this invasive buckthorn? And how do we

03:24:53.690 --> 03:24:57.335
enrich the soil health in this area, which is very poor?

03:24:58.266 --> 03:25:01.253
Yes, through you, Mr. Mayor, those are great questions. Um,

03:25:02.039 --> 03:25:06.080
so, so Soil health is important, and there

03:25:06.192 --> 03:25:09.864
will be— for each tree that's planted, typically what's done is there is

03:25:09.992 --> 03:25:13.743
a supplementation of the soil within the soil pit. That's typically a part

03:25:14.243 --> 03:25:17.959
of the planting. You want to make sure that your soil is

03:25:17.991 --> 03:25:20.556
going to support the trees that are being planted there.

03:25:21.839 --> 03:25:25.125
With respect to long-term invasive species management,

03:25:26.248 --> 03:25:29.745
at least for the first few years and several years, there would be invasive species

03:25:29.841 --> 03:25:34.017
management, and that's usually taken on by the developer. Beyond that,

03:25:34.114 --> 03:25:37.487
long-term, municipalities are typically

03:25:37.987 --> 03:25:41.952
stepping in, or should be stepping in, and we have examples of some municipalities

03:25:41.984 --> 03:25:46.048
that are developing invasive species management plans, recognizing it's a long-term

03:25:46.947 --> 03:25:50.352
commitment that has to be made to these natural areas in order to

03:25:50.721 --> 03:25:53.918
address impacts from invasive species, because they will

03:25:54.014 --> 03:25:57.873
come back. You've seen it in, I'm sure, many of your natural areas,

03:25:58.133 --> 03:26:01.013
and it's something that requires a long-term commitment.

03:26:01.820 --> 03:26:05.596
So yeah, I think that typically ends up becoming a municipality's

03:26:05.709 --> 03:26:09.598
responsibility, and there are municipalities that are developing invasive

03:26:10.098 --> 03:26:12.989
species management plans to be strategic about addressing those.

03:26:14.468 --> 03:26:18.374
No, thank you for that. I think if we can emphasize a commitment to stewarding

03:26:18.486 --> 03:26:22.535
that corridor with all this invasive

03:26:23.035 --> 03:26:26.727
species management, that actually I feel like is a net positive for this

03:26:26.824 --> 03:26:30.329
development and for the surrounding environment. So thank you.

03:26:30.780 --> 03:26:34.404
Thank you. Thank you. And I think last

03:26:34.904 --> 03:26:37.497
on my list I have is Caron, please. Thanks. And since we're on the subject

03:26:37.997 --> 03:26:41.888
of biodiversity, when this land was acquired, it was part of an annexation

03:26:42.049 --> 03:26:45.813
for— and this was agricultural, as you as you

03:26:46.313 --> 03:26:49.795
indicated in your report. We've also added a lot of uses,

03:26:49.907 --> 03:26:53.937
both the industrial use that is to

03:26:54.146 --> 03:26:57.775
the west, and we've added a high school and some

03:26:58.275 --> 03:27:01.645
baseball diamonds with high-intensity lighting. So you talked about

03:27:02.145 --> 03:27:05.322
the loss of migratory bird species and the fact that

03:27:05.822 --> 03:27:09.705
you haven't seen that as much biodiversity as

03:27:09.962 --> 03:27:13.861
what once may have been on the land. Do you think it's the adjacent

03:27:13.909 --> 03:27:17.407
land uses? Or like, we can't look at this land

03:27:17.920 --> 03:27:21.418
in isolation when you're doing an EIS. You have to look at all the

03:27:21.450 --> 03:27:24.900
other surrounding land uses as well. So to what degree

03:27:25.400 --> 03:27:28.959
did you, um, did you conclude that some of the other surrounding

03:27:29.472 --> 03:27:32.970
land uses may have impacted the biodiversity that's on this

03:27:33.066 --> 03:27:36.660
site? And then to previous questions, do you believe that we're actually bringing

03:27:36.692 --> 03:27:40.379
bringing back biodiversity through the ecological

03:27:40.636 --> 03:27:44.017
linkage. Yeah, through you, Mr. Mayor, um, just to clarify,

03:27:44.098 --> 03:27:47.445
I, I didn't, uh, look at or make any

03:27:47.782 --> 03:27:51.785
comments based on, um, any loss of

03:27:51.978 --> 03:27:55.065
wildlife as a result of any adjacent

03:27:55.516 --> 03:27:57.076
developments or impacts to them.

03:27:58.894 --> 03:28:02.160
We don't have a baseline of that to, to understand

03:28:02.206 --> 03:28:05.464
if there were any impacts from adjacent developments, um,

03:28:06.555 --> 03:28:09.894
and it's hard to say because of, of a lack of baseline studies.

03:28:10.231 --> 03:28:13.794
All we do know is what is on the site right now in terms of

03:28:14.294 --> 03:28:17.742
the biodiversity that's there and how it's functioning. And taking

03:28:17.790 --> 03:28:20.888
that into consideration and recognizing there is actually

03:28:21.388 --> 03:28:25.478
an opportunity to increase biodiversity through the restoration

03:28:25.978 --> 03:28:29.683
of this linkage, um, I do think there will

03:28:30.183 --> 03:28:31.531
will be an increase, because there can, because we know what the numbers and what

03:28:31.579 --> 03:28:35.159
the species that are there now and what the site can

03:28:35.946 --> 03:28:40.265
support in the future. So I do think there will be an improvement

03:28:40.426 --> 03:28:43.926
in biodiversity for the long term. But yeah,

03:28:43.942 --> 03:28:46.801
I can't say what it was before and compared to now.

03:28:47.234 --> 03:28:50.622
Okay. And what was your earliest baseline when you became involved? Was it

03:28:51.122 --> 03:28:54.640
2022, 2020? You've got measurements that we'll be able

03:28:55.140 --> 03:28:58.829
to compare to. Yes. Oh gosh,

03:28:58.909 --> 03:29:02.212
I, I, um, I want to say it was between

03:29:02.712 --> 03:29:06.245
2020 and 2022. I, I can't recall the first year we went out and started

03:29:06.745 --> 03:29:09.989
doing our surveys on site, um, but I,

03:29:10.666 --> 03:29:14.084
yeah, I, I, I can't remember exactly the first date we

03:29:14.101 --> 03:29:15.899
went on site for our surveys. Sorry.

03:29:18.850 --> 03:29:21.298
No problem. And I think that is it.

03:29:21.978 --> 03:29:24.118
So thank you very much for your presentation.

03:29:25.718 --> 03:29:28.529
Okay, moving right along. We got, is it Jake?

03:29:30.210 --> 03:29:33.294
Jake. Jake Hawkins from Counterpoint.

03:29:34.973 --> 03:29:38.314
And Jeremiah Vander Laan from Kiwi Newton,

03:29:38.459 --> 03:29:42.353
I think, is going to be ready to join us by video afterwards. So Jake,

03:29:42.500 --> 03:29:46.098
over to you. So good evening, Mr. Mayor. Members of council,

03:29:46.114 --> 03:29:49.629
my name is Jake Hawkins and I'm a civil engineer working for Counterpoint

03:29:49.709 --> 03:29:53.898
and on behalf of Home Opportunities in support of the subject residential development.

03:29:54.765 --> 03:29:58.521
I'd like to briefly share how the stormwater management strategy integrated

03:29:58.537 --> 03:30:02.357
with the environmental linkage provides a necessary storm outlet

03:30:02.405 --> 03:30:05.920
to permit development of affordable housing while protecting Guelph's

03:30:06.420 --> 03:30:10.173
groundwater, utilizing the unique Mi'kmaq topography and

03:30:10.673 --> 03:30:14.815
high infiltration rate soils, a naturalized low-impact infiltration

03:30:14.911 --> 03:30:18.509
basin is being designed in consultation with municipal engineering

03:30:18.557 --> 03:30:22.476
staff to meet MECP and city standards around

03:30:22.976 --> 03:30:26.540
stormwater quality, quantity, and water balance. The proposed

03:30:26.572 --> 03:30:30.267
basin will infiltrate 100-year post-development flows with no runoff

03:30:30.767 --> 03:30:33.351
being directed to municipal infrastructure. Upstream,

03:30:33.463 --> 03:30:36.918
stormwater quality devices and low-impact development measures will be

03:30:37.418 --> 03:30:40.204
utilized to pretreat runoff to remove suspended solids,

03:30:40.574 --> 03:30:44.086
minimizing the need— the need for maintenance. The infiltration

03:30:44.118 --> 03:30:47.410
basin will be designed with no obstructions that would prevent

03:30:47.910 --> 03:30:51.170
wildlife crossings, and it would be equipped with a naturalized access

03:30:51.299 --> 03:30:53.250
that could be utilized for maintenance when needed.

03:30:57.036 --> 03:31:00.498
The natural ecological characteristics make the

03:31:00.998 --> 03:31:04.703
environmental linkage ideal for stormwater water management and groundwater recharge

03:31:05.203 --> 03:31:09.075
needed for this affordable housing development. We look forward to continuing close

03:31:09.575 --> 03:31:12.672
collaboration with city staff and to bring, to bring this project to life.

03:31:13.511 --> 03:31:16.803
Thank you, and I'll take any technical questions related to

03:31:17.303 --> 03:31:20.783
stormwater management. Great, thank you

03:31:21.283 --> 03:31:23.572
very much. Is there any questions on that?

03:31:25.785 --> 03:31:29.088
I guess it was very clear, so no questions. So thank you very much.

