03:35:32.913 --> 03:35:37.072
with, as well as persistent transportation expenses,

03:35:38.132 --> 03:35:42.900
the economic uncertainty that continues to challenge

03:35:43.400 --> 03:35:47.477
us all with the impact of trade pressures continuing

03:35:47.525 --> 03:35:51.218
to impact working families. These are challenges that

03:35:51.234 --> 03:35:55.537
no single organization, industry, or government alone can

03:35:55.569 --> 03:35:58.675
solve. That's why I'm encouraged by what I've seen

03:35:58.691 --> 03:36:01.814
here today and in the public meeting. We've had

03:36:01.846 --> 03:36:05.936
representatives from nonprofit organizations, we've had representatives

03:36:06.436 --> 03:36:10.342
from First Nation communities and businesses coming together

03:36:10.729 --> 03:36:12.262
around this shared opportunity.

03:36:14.152 --> 03:36:17.325
In a time when it's often easier to focus on what divides

03:36:17.825 --> 03:36:21.938
us, it's really refreshing to see this diverse group come together and be united

03:36:22.438 --> 03:36:26.097
by a common objective of strengthening our community and creating opportunities

03:36:26.129 --> 03:36:30.173
for people who live and work here. This type of collaboration

03:36:30.365 --> 03:36:34.040
is rare, but it demonstrates what's possible when community leaders

03:36:34.072 --> 03:36:37.152
like yourselves and representatives come together,

03:36:37.922 --> 03:36:41.533
put their individual interests aside, and work collectively to resolve

03:36:42.033 --> 03:36:45.801
a long-term, uh, community need. That's why tonight,

03:36:45.945 --> 03:36:49.555
on behalf of Lillooet, I'm so happy to provide support

03:36:50.055 --> 03:36:53.731
for this proposal and urge the council to

03:36:53.941 --> 03:36:57.141
approve the proposal that is before them, or to

03:36:57.158 --> 03:37:01.268
work diligently, as the mayor had mentioned, to getting to a yes.

03:37:02.030 --> 03:37:04.347
Thank you so much for your time and attention.

03:37:06.831 --> 03:37:10.612
Okay, and thank you, Sean, for waiting and giving us your presentation.

03:37:10.726 --> 03:37:14.143
Is there any follow-up questions from my colleagues? Okay, Councillor Chu.

03:37:16.194 --> 03:37:20.079
Thank you. Through you, Mr. Mayor. Thank you, Shawn, for just sharing and

03:37:20.177 --> 03:37:22.157
reminding us of those testimonies of your employees.

03:37:23.560 --> 03:37:27.612
Can you just paint me a picture of some of the struggles maybe Linamar

03:37:27.628 --> 03:37:30.853
has with retaining employees in town

03:37:30.886 --> 03:37:34.425
these days compared to having folks be

03:37:34.925 --> 03:37:38.737
out of town and just the decrease in productivity

03:37:39.237 --> 03:37:40.723
that causes on your employees?

03:37:42.680 --> 03:37:46.081
Absolutely. Varen shared with me personally in the last story a

03:37:46.129 --> 03:37:48.945
number of times where— Sean,

03:37:49.609 --> 03:37:50.985
it's Mayor Guthrie. Sorry to interrupt.

03:37:53.206 --> 03:37:56.629
If you could please just answer the question by referring back

03:37:57.129 --> 03:38:00.651
to the report about the planning or the alternative motions that are

03:38:01.151 --> 03:38:04.992
being presented for the actual buildings

03:38:05.041 --> 03:38:08.673
that may be approved by council. I think that's where I think the answer

03:38:09.173 --> 03:38:13.333
needs to lie. Not in the question really that came from Councillor Chu.

03:38:14.489 --> 03:38:18.552
I think I'll allow that to happen, but we've already heard testimonies,

03:38:18.584 --> 03:38:22.116
has been corrected, and we understand those stories. So I

03:38:22.616 --> 03:38:25.954
think if we could, if we could point you back to the report,

03:38:26.371 --> 03:38:29.551
the alternative motions that are trying to get to a yes, and how that would

03:38:29.567 --> 03:38:32.633
help your employees, that, that would be okay with me,

03:38:33.115 --> 03:38:36.725
and you can go from there, okay? I'll do my best to answer that

03:38:37.225 --> 03:38:40.845
question in the manner that you suggest, Mr. Mayor. Yes,

03:38:41.758 --> 03:38:45.798
maximizing the density that is in the proposal and

03:38:46.007 --> 03:38:50.158
ensuring that the project remains viable is absolutely,

03:38:50.575 --> 03:38:54.038
I would say, the most important thing from our

03:38:54.538 --> 03:38:57.773
perspective to ensure that, that employees,

03:38:57.869 --> 03:39:01.059
whether they be machine operators

03:39:01.818 --> 03:39:05.510
or even more highly skilled and better paid

03:39:06.107 --> 03:39:09.865
are all able to access this initiative.

03:39:09.913 --> 03:39:13.584
So I think that's really critical for us to ensure that

03:39:13.681 --> 03:39:16.704
the, that the project is viable. And,

03:39:17.254 --> 03:39:20.082
and so that's important for us to keep in mind this evening.

03:39:22.360 --> 03:39:24.763
Councillor Chu, you still have the floor. Yeah, no, thank you.

03:39:25.721 --> 03:39:29.499
Okay, thank you. And Sean, I see no other further

03:39:29.999 --> 03:39:32.231
questions, so again, thank you for sticking around. We appreciate it.

03:39:35.043 --> 03:39:38.609
Thank you. And Valerie, uh, unfortunately is not

03:39:39.109 --> 03:39:42.852
available for us, uh, to speak tonight, uh, so that brings

03:39:43.352 --> 03:39:46.794
us to Laura Muir, who is here in person, and then we'll

03:39:46.826 --> 03:39:50.044
be ending with Hugh Whiteley, who will join us by video.

03:39:50.546 --> 03:39:53.722
So Laura, come on up. And, uh,

03:39:53.738 --> 03:39:55.759
your 5 minutes will start whenever you're ready. Okay.

03:39:59.638 --> 03:40:03.263
Oh, thank you for the opportunity to speak. Mayor Guthrie and members

03:40:03.311 --> 03:40:07.440
of council, city staff have reviewed the 104 studies submitted

03:40:07.472 --> 03:40:11.168
for this proposed development and have recommended refusal.

03:40:11.794 --> 03:40:15.064
Council should accept that recommendation. This application

03:40:15.257 --> 03:40:19.380
asks you to approve a development today while relying on a assurances that

03:40:19.880 --> 03:40:23.793
serious unresolved issues will somehow be fixed later. This is not

03:40:24.293 --> 03:40:28.382
responsible planning. It is not a risk council should pass on to future residents

03:40:29.008 --> 03:40:33.084
or the broader community as well. The alternative staff recommendations

03:40:33.116 --> 03:40:36.646
do not appear to solve the inherent problems with this development, just put

03:40:37.146 --> 03:40:41.284
them off to a later date. First, the concerns about air quality and

03:40:41.380 --> 03:40:45.360
public health are serious. This site is located near significant

03:40:45.860 --> 03:40:49.160
industrial operations. Where there is uncertainty about public

03:40:49.660 --> 03:40:53.745
health, the precautionary principle must apply. Future residents

03:40:53.793 --> 03:40:57.176
should not be placed in a position where their health depends on assumptions,

03:40:57.240 --> 03:41:00.623
deferred studies, or mitigation measures that may

03:41:01.123 --> 03:41:04.920
not have proven to resolve the problem. Second, noise and livability

03:41:04.952 --> 03:41:08.384
concerns are not minor technical details to be dealt

03:41:08.884 --> 03:41:12.424
with after approval. The concerns deal directly with whether people can

03:41:12.472 --> 03:41:16.918
have a reasonable quality of life on on this site. Council should not approve housing

03:41:17.304 --> 03:41:21.207
where there remain significant questions about sleep, outdoor enjoyment,

03:41:21.303 --> 03:41:24.997
and the basic quality of life. The Burnside peer review found

03:41:25.497 --> 03:41:29.720
that a 4-meter-high noise wall would be required to mitigate transportation

03:41:29.816 --> 03:41:33.221
noise levels. That is a clear indication that this site is

03:41:33.286 --> 03:41:37.221
facing major compatibility problems, not routine design

03:41:37.721 --> 03:41:41.284
adjustments. The backup alarm on a truck is 55 decibels.

03:41:41.413 --> 03:41:44.978
Is this an acceptable noise if you're trying to sleep or enjoy your yard?

03:41:45.653 --> 03:41:49.090
Third, the open space and community design is inadequate.

03:41:49.492 --> 03:41:52.736
The proposal builds over most of the site, leaving very little

03:41:53.266 --> 03:41:56.896
and limited usable green space and raising concerns about shadow

03:41:56.976 --> 03:42:00.622
impacts on outdoor amenity areas. Residents deserve

03:42:00.750 --> 03:42:04.525
more than a place to sleep. They deserve a neighborhood that supports

03:42:05.025 --> 03:42:08.732
dignity, health, and community life. This proposal does not demonstrate that

03:42:08.780 --> 03:42:12.377
once people move in that they will enjoy the same quality of life and

03:42:12.473 --> 03:42:15.267
use and enjoyment of their property as other city residents.

03:42:16.022 --> 03:42:19.907
Stormwater and ecological concerns are equally troubling. The site is

03:42:20.084 --> 03:42:23.231
too dense. There's no room for stormwater management on site,

03:42:23.616 --> 03:42:27.229
so the current design relies on directing stormwater onto the proposed

03:42:27.261 --> 03:42:31.179
city-owned ecological link. That area would be flooded

03:42:31.679 --> 03:42:36.057
during major rainfall events. This is not acceptable for land being offered

03:42:36.557 --> 03:42:39.763
as a replacement for an existing functioning habitat for endangered bats

03:42:39.843 --> 03:42:43.132
and the Eastern Meadowlark. The Meadowlark is a ground nesting bird

03:42:43.632 --> 03:42:47.224
that needs dry ground, not flood water. Council should not accept a plan

03:42:47.724 --> 03:42:51.267
that shifts unresolved engineering and ecological problems onto

03:42:51.395 --> 03:42:55.310
public land and future taxpayers. These are not small details

03:42:55.810 --> 03:42:59.178
to be worked out later. They are fundamental reasons reasons to refuse the application

03:42:59.678 --> 03:43:03.160
now. We all recognize the urgent need for affordable housing,

03:43:03.593 --> 03:43:06.692
but affordability cannot come at the expense of livability,

03:43:06.724 --> 03:43:09.438
public health, or the quality of life of future residents.

03:43:10.417 --> 03:43:13.211
Council's decisions tonight will affect future residents,

03:43:13.725 --> 03:43:17.097
neighboring businesses, the surrounding community, and the public interest.

03:43:17.627 --> 03:43:20.886
Growth, but growth must be responsible, transparent, and based

03:43:20.998 --> 03:43:24.435
on clear evidence that the proposal works. not on promises

03:43:24.451 --> 03:43:28.224
that major problems will be solved later. Staff have done their job.

03:43:28.545 --> 03:43:31.837
They have identified serious unresolved concerns and

03:43:32.337 --> 03:43:36.027
they have recommended refusal. Now Council must do its job: protect future

03:43:36.527 --> 03:43:39.528
residents, protect the public interest, and uphold good planning,

03:43:39.624 --> 03:43:43.253
which includes a walkable community. Do not approve a development

03:43:43.753 --> 03:43:47.717
that is not ready, with unresolved issues, and not appropriate for this site. I strongly

03:43:47.749 --> 03:43:51.811
urge Council to support the staff's recommendation and refuse this application.

03:43:51.924 --> 03:43:55.857
Thank you. Great, thank you, Laura, for coming and your presentation.

03:43:55.922 --> 03:43:59.599
Is there any questions as a follow-up? There is

03:43:59.695 --> 03:44:02.938
not. Thank you very much. And Hugh Whiteley will now join

03:44:03.438 --> 03:44:03.645
us by video.

03:44:26.028 --> 03:44:28.517
Hello, Hugh, it's Mayor Guthrie. Can you hear me okay?

03:44:43.380 --> 03:44:47.404
It's interesting. He isn't coming up on my screen here at

03:44:47.904 --> 03:44:49.967
all, but I see him up there. That's interesting.

03:44:51.526 --> 03:44:54.757
Yeah, Hugh, do you want to maybe just turn your camera on?

03:44:56.560 --> 03:44:59.834
Or you could keep it off and just hit the mute

03:45:00.334 --> 03:45:01.235
button to unmute.

03:45:16.927 --> 03:45:21.343
So, Hugh, again, just do you want to— I'm

03:45:21.843 --> 03:45:25.534
wondering if you can hear me okay. Just let me continue to

03:45:26.034 --> 03:45:28.135
let you try, and I'll just confer with the clerks for a moment.

03:46:09.547 --> 03:46:11.628
I think I've successfully unmuted.

03:46:14.630 --> 03:46:16.780
So no one touch anything,

03:46:17.394 --> 03:46:19.270
okay? Uh,

03:46:21.614 --> 03:46:25.505
we can keep the camera off. Don't press any other buttons. We can

03:46:26.005 --> 03:46:29.978
keep— we can hear you great. So I'll the microphone over to you. Go for

03:46:30.027 --> 03:46:32.369
it. Greetings, Mayor,

03:46:33.556 --> 03:46:37.294
members of council, and members of the public. I have

03:46:37.390 --> 03:46:41.480
an alternative view to almost

03:46:41.980 --> 03:46:45.234
everything that's been expressed this evening as

03:46:45.427 --> 03:46:49.485
related to this site. I am very

03:46:49.533 --> 03:46:53.159
much interested in the procedure for

03:46:53.672 --> 03:46:57.394
developing affordable housing on a very different

03:46:57.894 --> 03:47:01.401
economic and basis, but this is not

03:47:01.901 --> 03:47:03.722
the site that it should be developed on.

03:47:06.210 --> 03:47:10.082
In the years immediately after annexation,

03:47:10.679 --> 03:47:13.793
the city did excellent site planning

03:47:14.455 --> 03:47:17.667
for the property that is under discussion

03:47:17.780 --> 03:47:21.542
tonight and made the very wise

03:47:21.655 --> 03:47:25.191
decision that the Garibaldi

03:47:25.290 --> 03:47:28.980
property, which is the general block

03:47:29.480 --> 03:47:33.934
of land along Clare Road that's— we're discussing had

03:47:34.354 --> 03:47:38.160
a portion which was well suited to industrial development,

03:47:39.432 --> 03:47:42.154
and that is the western portion.

03:47:43.217 --> 03:47:46.780
And the city equally well defined

03:47:47.280 --> 03:47:51.354
that the boulder

03:47:51.386 --> 03:47:55.369
pavement Outwash Valley that lay

03:47:55.594 --> 03:47:58.998
east of that was a very

03:47:59.498 --> 03:48:02.289
interesting natural feature and, uh,

03:48:02.594 --> 03:48:06.673
continued south in other aspects

03:48:07.173 --> 03:48:10.028
of natural heritage right to Maltby.

03:48:11.345 --> 03:48:15.166
And so the city decided very wisely that

03:48:15.262 --> 03:48:18.698
that whole complex from Clare Road with

03:48:18.714 --> 03:48:23.123
the Outwash Valley through to Maltby should

03:48:23.155 --> 03:48:25.488
be retained as a natural area.

03:48:26.952 --> 03:48:29.688
And they were very serious about that.

03:48:31.660 --> 03:48:35.729
They were sufficiently serious that they convinced the

03:48:35.793 --> 03:48:39.927
landowner of the Garibaldi property to sever the

03:48:40.427 --> 03:48:43.807
property into two portions. The western

03:48:43.986 --> 03:48:47.500
portion was what was purchased

03:48:48.000 --> 03:48:51.122
by Denso and is the developable part of

03:48:51.138 --> 03:48:54.544
the property. The eastern outwash valley

03:48:55.948 --> 03:48:59.143
was severed with very irregular boundaries

03:48:59.336 --> 03:49:02.402
to define from top

03:49:02.902 --> 03:49:06.888
of bank on one side to top of bank on the other and was

03:49:07.163 --> 03:49:10.600
given P-1

03:49:10.713 --> 03:49:13.108
conservation zoning as parkland.

03:49:15.022 --> 03:49:19.102
Those allocations of which part of the property

03:49:19.118 --> 03:49:23.181
was developable and which part was parkland are

03:49:23.615 --> 03:49:27.743
well founded on the environmental

03:49:28.243 --> 03:49:31.903
studies that were done at the time. And it's my position

03:49:32.403 --> 03:49:35.806
they should be retained. And for that reason,

03:49:36.882 --> 03:49:40.020
I am advocating that Council follow

03:49:40.845 --> 03:49:45.309
staff's general direction and refuse the application

03:49:46.004 --> 03:49:50.920
before you tonight. My anticipation

03:49:50.952 --> 03:49:53.987
is that if you make that decision,

03:49:55.396 --> 03:49:59.810
what will become available is a final conversion

03:50:00.004 --> 03:50:03.238
of that outwash valley into one

03:50:03.738 --> 03:50:07.272
of Guelph's premier Natural Area Parklands.

03:50:10.476 --> 03:50:14.165
Why the city did not purchase it at the same

03:50:14.295 --> 03:50:17.745
time as the Denso property

03:50:17.761 --> 03:50:21.421
was purchased for industrial development remains a

03:50:21.502 --> 03:50:25.505
great mystery. They retained— the city

03:50:25.537 --> 03:50:28.943
retained that zoning for almost 30

03:50:29.106 --> 03:50:32.572
years. And never in that period

03:50:32.909 --> 03:50:36.819
acted to safeguard it for perpetuity.

03:50:38.037 --> 03:50:42.059
And if you turn down the application tonight,

03:50:43.085 --> 03:50:47.668
I suggest that the opportunity is then available to negotiate

03:50:48.168 --> 03:50:51.434
with the landowner and finally bring it into public

03:50:51.498 --> 03:50:56.369
ownership and begin to develop a really impressive

03:50:56.963 --> 03:51:00.977
biodiverse large area

03:51:01.477 --> 03:51:06.533
for human to natural contact,

03:51:06.662 --> 03:51:10.114
uh, content for the future.

03:51:11.173 --> 03:51:15.862
And I would suggest that that's

03:51:15.990 --> 03:51:20.021
the opportunity that the council should consider tonight.

03:51:24.658 --> 03:51:28.289
Well, thank you, Hugh, and I'm glad we were able to

03:51:28.789 --> 03:51:32.526
hear, hear you, and thank you for the presentation. I don't see

03:51:33.026 --> 03:51:35.396
any of my colleagues with a hand up, so that we don't have any questions

03:51:35.428 --> 03:51:38.161
for you, but thank you again for joining us. Very good.

03:51:40.041 --> 03:51:43.913
Great. Okay, um, we have no more

03:51:44.459 --> 03:51:47.984
delegations, so, um, we are taking a break.

03:51:48.988 --> 03:51:53.356
We have to. 'Cause I can't feel my legs. So it's

03:51:53.856 --> 03:51:57.145
a quarter to 9, basically. We're gonna come back here

03:51:57.354 --> 03:52:00.967
in 10 minutes. Okay, 5 minutes to 9. I want everyone back

03:52:01.467 --> 03:52:04.740
in their seat and we will then move on from there, okay? Thank you very

04:06:39.500 --> 04:06:42.598
Okay, everybody, I'd like to call the meeting back to order, please.

04:06:43.420 --> 04:06:46.517
To my friends online, if you could throw up your camera, that would be great.

04:06:47.324 --> 04:06:50.470
Thank you. Councillor Ull, Councillor Keating, I see you.

04:06:51.906 --> 04:06:54.907
Billings, thank you so much. And Hauser, I see you.

04:06:55.313 --> 04:06:58.900
We have quorum here. We're going to continue. And so

04:06:58.917 --> 04:06:59.975
thank you to the delegations.

04:07:01.900 --> 04:07:05.339
Let's, let's go back to our staff

04:07:05.791 --> 04:07:09.118
and ask some follow-up questions if need be,

04:07:09.134 --> 04:07:13.116
to them. And then we can look to entertain

04:07:14.175 --> 04:07:18.527
whatever motions. I know Councillor Goller, you would like to move motions,

04:07:18.543 --> 04:07:22.075
I think so. But let's just start with questions.

04:07:22.284 --> 04:07:25.543
I think it's important to start with questions today,

04:07:26.073 --> 04:07:29.269
then to maybe table some things just to get some more clarity. So Councillor

04:07:29.769 --> 04:07:32.721
Keating, I see your hand first. You go right ahead. And then Alt after you.

04:07:34.339 --> 04:07:38.012
Thank you. Through you to staff, something I had not

04:07:38.512 --> 04:07:42.340
thought about originally is the bottleneck design in terms

04:07:42.453 --> 04:07:46.019
of emergency services and things. And I was wondering if,

04:07:47.224 --> 04:07:49.860
if it's something that needs to be decided upon now, or if it's something that

04:07:50.360 --> 04:07:53.652
gets decided upon later at site plan in terms

04:07:54.152 --> 04:07:58.816
of having an extra road connection potentially at the south

04:07:58.915 --> 04:08:02.060
end of that. Development somewhere or off to the side?

04:08:02.125 --> 04:08:02.919
I don't know what would be feasible.

04:08:06.060 --> 04:08:09.879
Yeah, through the mayor to Councillor Keaton. Uh, good question. Um, I know that's been

04:08:10.379 --> 04:08:12.973
flagged internally, and that would be something that we would look at at site plan,

04:08:13.730 --> 04:08:15.535
um, when it comes in at that time.

04:08:18.472 --> 04:08:19.404
Great, thank you very much.

04:08:21.717 --> 04:08:24.804
Great, thanks, Councillor Keaton. Um, that was a very good

04:08:25.304 --> 04:08:29.001
question. Thank you. Uh, Alt, please. Thank you,

04:08:29.017 --> 04:08:31.965
Mayor Guthrie. 2 questions, I think, for 2 different staff people.

04:08:32.526 --> 04:08:36.436
Um, first of all, I believe that we have somebody from Finance, Miss Odawa,

04:08:36.452 --> 04:08:39.673
or somebody else present, and if so, it's a

04:08:40.173 --> 04:08:43.407
question for Finance. Yeah, no problem. She's on her way down. You can go ahead.

04:08:44.080 --> 04:08:47.846
Okay, thank you. I'll wait till she gets settled. You never know.

04:08:48.470 --> 04:08:51.034
Um, once she's there, if you just let me know.

04:08:51.355 --> 04:08:54.742
Thanks. She settled. Perfect,

04:08:54.983 --> 04:08:58.644
thank you. Uh, thanks, Miss Odawa, for being present. Um,

04:08:58.820 --> 04:09:02.931
I've got, uh, a multifaceted question, but I'm concerned about

04:09:03.431 --> 04:09:06.688
the, uh, the, uh, uh,

04:09:06.704 --> 04:09:10.734
forgiving, I guess, of DC's— its impact on city finances.

04:09:11.184 --> 04:09:14.620
In one sense, on the tax rate, because I don't know precisely

04:09:14.636 --> 04:09:18.313
what kind of money we are talking about and over what length of

04:09:18.409 --> 04:09:21.973
period. I'm concerned about reserves, and I,

04:09:22.182 --> 04:09:25.665
I did not hear that HomeOptions

04:09:25.713 --> 04:09:30.272
will actually pay the DCs ever. So under what conditions

04:09:30.866 --> 04:09:33.948
would you be comfortable, uh,

04:09:34.301 --> 04:09:38.217
with the deferral of the DCs to a future date,

04:09:38.715 --> 04:09:42.311
if that's even possible? And in your professional opinion,

04:09:42.985 --> 04:09:46.179
can the city afford that? Because it strikes

04:09:46.679 --> 04:09:52.386
me that there could be a potential of a tax implication.

04:09:52.599 --> 04:09:56.019
Through the Mayor, thank you for the question. So just to

04:09:56.519 --> 04:09:59.939
start, there's— the province created a development charge exemption for

04:10:00.439 --> 04:10:03.795
nonprofit housing providers, and so that

04:10:04.295 --> 04:10:06.563
exemption is based on the status of the developer,

04:10:07.563 --> 04:10:11.577
which is a nonprofit corporation or co-op that's— the primary

04:10:11.674 --> 04:10:15.464
object is to provide housing. So that's a broad exemption

04:10:15.721 --> 04:10:18.866
that doesn't require that any of the units be sold or

04:10:18.930 --> 04:10:22.764
rented at or under the province's affordability threshold.

04:10:24.000 --> 04:10:27.192
That stands in contrast to the Affordable

04:10:27.692 --> 04:10:31.380
Housing DC exemption that's available to for-profit

04:10:31.880 --> 04:10:35.311
providers that have to commit through an agreement

04:10:35.375 --> 04:10:39.273
registered on title to maintaining the sale

04:10:39.402 --> 04:10:43.089
of that affordable unit or the rental of that affordable unit at

04:10:43.874 --> 04:10:47.321
an affordable rate as defined by the province for 25

04:10:47.821 --> 04:10:50.880
years. And so that's the one thing.

04:10:51.008 --> 04:10:55.497
So if this development comes in through a nonprofit

04:10:56.635 --> 04:11:00.162
organization that meets the criteria that is defined in

04:11:00.662 --> 04:11:03.497
the DC Act for nonprofit exemption,

04:11:04.170 --> 04:11:08.129
there is no further requirements

04:11:08.629 --> 04:11:11.097
beyond that to guarantee affordable housing.

04:11:12.578 --> 04:11:15.831
And then there is a property tax and rate impact

04:11:16.413 --> 04:11:20.410
for DC exemptions, and there is no ability

04:11:20.426 --> 04:11:23.721
for the city to enforce the kind of voluntary

04:11:23.834 --> 04:11:27.595
payment mechanism that was maybe described earlier

04:11:27.627 --> 04:11:30.355
this evening if it's a nonprofit.

04:11:31.474 --> 04:11:34.860
Exemption. Um, and then with respect to your question

04:11:34.908 --> 04:11:38.295
around the status of the city's reserves, so the city's DC

04:11:38.795 --> 04:11:42.067
reserve funds were overcommitted at the end of 2025. Uh,

04:11:42.227 --> 04:11:46.240
they continue to be overcommitted throughout the forecast, and we're currently in

04:11:46.740 --> 04:11:49.722
the process of deferring out, um, over $100 million,

04:11:50.798 --> 04:11:54.746
um, of capital through the next, uh, budget update to be brought to

04:11:55.246 --> 04:11:58.326
council in 2026. 7, uh, for 2027.

04:11:59.065 --> 04:12:02.533
And that is because we, um, have had low DC

04:12:03.033 --> 04:12:06.547
collections, so we don't have the money in, uh, the reserves to,

04:12:07.334 --> 04:12:10.722
uh, just deal with that. And then the other important piece is that the city's

04:12:11.222 --> 04:12:14.864
policy is to fund DC exemptions from other sources,

04:12:15.523 --> 04:12:18.766
um, and the reason for that is because not funding them will drive up

04:12:19.266 --> 04:12:22.812
DC rates. Um, so that's because the exemptions don't lower

04:12:22.844 --> 04:12:26.198
the cost of building, uh, the infrastructure that's

04:12:26.698 --> 04:12:29.890
required to enable and serve growth. Someone has to pay for

04:12:30.390 --> 04:12:34.657
it, um, and it's either the city's property tax and ratepayers or its future development.

04:12:35.828 --> 04:12:39.824
Um, and so then I guess my, my last comment would be that approving

04:12:39.857 --> 04:12:42.954
the requested zoning changes or changes, planning changes

04:12:43.066 --> 04:12:46.597
without conditions would leave the council and the community with

04:12:46.613 --> 04:12:50.046
no guarantee of an enduring public benefit

04:12:50.546 --> 04:12:54.376
by way of housing that will be and will remain affordable in return

04:12:54.489 --> 04:12:58.672
for a public investment in those DC exemptions. So hopefully

04:12:58.704 --> 04:12:59.882
that answers your question.

